{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17433","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17433","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17433","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahasra","familyName":"Kondapalli","name":"Kondapalli, Sahasra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lara","familyName":"Radovanovic","name":"Radovanovic, Lara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadi","familyName":"Palnitkar","name":"Palnitkar, Aadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xiaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LADBench: A Benchmark for Logical Fault Detection in Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:32:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:21:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T03:51:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:36:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision Language Models (VLMs) excel at visual question answering and semantic grounding, but their capacity for autonomous logical reasoning remains underexplored. Existing anomaly benchmarks emphasize visual errors or direct prompting rather than the physical and social common sense needed for open-world deployment. To address this, we introduce LAD-bench, a benchmark of more than 1,000 curated synthetic images with logical anomalies across four domains: Residential, Urban, Collaborative, and Nature. We further propose a Tiered Prompting Protocol based on progressive disclosure, which measures how much explicit assistance a model needs to localize and reason about a logical fault. Evaluating leading foundation models reveals substantial weaknesses: even the best achieves only 70.11% overall accuracy, showing that implicit logical fault detection remains unsolved. Crucially, models often fail to identify anomalies even after receiving explicit hints in deeper tiers. By surfacing these limitations in sequential multimodal reasoning, LAD-Bench offers a rigorous framework for advancing the safety, reliability, and cognitive alignment of autonomous visual systems. Dataset and Code: https://huggingface.co/datasets/SahasraK/LADBench"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the IEEE International Conference on Development and Learning (ICDL 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17433","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:48Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:18:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyue","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuquan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaye","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiaye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbo","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Siyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Brewing to Resolution: Tracing the Internal Lifecycle of Code Reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:06:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-26T08:06:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T00:31:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-11T03:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:12:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Standard accuracy metrics cannot explain why LLMs handle variable tracking but fail on semantically equivalent loops. We study an internal lifecycle of code reasoning in which models first brew the answer, making it linearly recoverable many layers before it becomes self-decodable, and then diverge into one of four resolution outcomes: Resolved, Overprocessed, Misresolved, or Unresolved. Understanding this lifecycle matters because similar task accuracies can mask fundamentally different failure modes that surface-level evaluation cannot detect. We introduce a dual diagnostic framework pairing layer-wise linear probing with Context-Stripped Decoding (CSD) and apply it to six code-reasoning task families across 16 models spanning Qwen, Llama, and DeepSeek architectures. All four outcomes carry substantial mass in every task family: overall Resolved is only 41.5%, with multiple tasks below 30%. Controlled sweeps over structure, depth, and operators expose task-specific failure bottlenecks: Function Call Resolved plunges from 61.1% to 2.5% as call depth increases from one to three. Across architectures and scales, the brewing scaffold remains stable, with normalized brewing duration 24-42% across all 16 models, while resolution success varies with capability. This indicates that the scaffold is a stable empirical regularity across the tested decoder-only Transformer families, whereas resolution success covaries with capability, scale, and training. Code: https://github.com/euyis1019/llm-brewing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiufeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongqian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rongqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sizhe","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Sizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Tung","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Chia-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Z.","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, David Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PDAGENT-BENCH: Characterizing, Grounding, and Architecting LLM/VLM Agents for VLSI Physical Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:54:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T19:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:04:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-05T03:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:28:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:41:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:15:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-08T02:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:17:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-10-13T03:21:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models and vision-language models have shown remarkable success in the front-end design of Very Large-Scale Integrated Circuits, yet their capabilities for VLSI physical design remain significantly underexplored. The primary cause is the lack of standardized benchmarks for evaluating agentic physical design workflows that require high-dimensional, multi-stage optimization under strict design constraints, coordinated interaction with diverse Electronic Design Automation tools, and iterative refinement. This work introduces PDAGENT-BENCH, a comprehensive and multi-dimensional benchmark for evaluating LLM/VLM-based agents across the physical design stack. PDAGENT-BENCH integrates both task-level assessment and workflow-level execution. The benchmark suite contains 353 curated problems that combine conceptual questions with real-world industrial artifacts, with expert-validated references and executable solutions. In addition, the benchmark provides a unified, human-aligned agentic physical design workflow framework that enables closed-loop evaluation of holistic physical design in realistic EDA environments. Experiments on 11 state-of-the-art models reveal that while modern LLMs/VLMs perform competitively on conceptual tasks, they remain substantially limited in tool-centric execution (e.g., 42.2% on Innovus script generation) and long-horizon, multi-stage reasoning. Our studies further show that human-skill-enhanced agentic workflows significantly improve end-to-end physical design performance. PDAGENT-BENCH establishes a standardized, reproducible, and realistic evaluation framework for advancing LLM/VLM-driven holistic physical design automation. To ensure full reproducibility, we release \\textsc{PDAgent-Bench} at https://github.com/fenggev56/PDAGENT-BENCH."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pianran","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Pianran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongliang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linna","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Linna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinAcumen: Financial Multimodal Reasoning via Self-Evolving Experience Memory Harness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T05:24:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:14:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-23T12:00:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:55:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-07T07:47:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-12T14:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:41:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:27:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial multimodal reasoning requires agents to coordinate numerical computation, retrieval, visual interpretation, and temporal grounding across heterogeneous evidence sources. Existing tool-augmented agents improve execution fidelity, yet remain largely stateless across episodes, repeatedly rediscovering reasoning strategies and failure patterns. In high-stakes financial settings, this leads to unreliable tool routing, noisy retrieval, and hallucination-prone reasoning. We present FinAcumen, a financial reasoning agent framework centered on selective experience memory for tool-augmented multimodal reasoning. FinAcumen accumulates financially grounded reasoning experience from prior trajectories, distilling successful strategies and failure-derived cautionary rules into a persistent memory bank. During inference, retrieved experiences condition reasoning only when semantic relevance exceeds a calibrated threshold, while irrelevant memory is explicitly suppressed through a fallback mechanism. A deterministic financial tool environment further grounds numerical computation, retrieval, visual decoding, and answer verification. Across four financial multimodal reasoning benchmarks, FinAcumen consistently improves a frozen 8B vision-language model over finance-specialized models and approaches leading proprietary general-purpose models. Further analysis shows that selective experience activation improves reasoning reliability under retrieval uncertainty. Our code is available at https://github.com/CamelliaLilium/FinAcumen."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:52:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaditya","familyName":"Pai","name":"Pai, Aaditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PARSE: Provenance-Aware Retrieval Sanitization for Professional Domain LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:29:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-21T14:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-22T00:55:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-12T21:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T00:51:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt injection defenses evaluated on synthetic benchmarks do not generalize to real enterprise documents, which are longer, denser, and interleave legitimate authority language with factual content. We demonstrate this gap with a benchmark of 122 tasks across five professional domains (financial, legal, medical, scientific, DevOps) built on real retrieved documents -- actual SEC filings, Federal Register rules, PubMed abstracts, arXiv papers, and GitHub postmortems -- paired with LLM-generated tasks and camouflaged payloads that were not human-validated. Paraphrasing, the strongest defense on synthetic benchmarks, shows no statistically significant attack success rate reduction on real documents (p=0.500) while degrading utility from 91.8% to 82.8%. We introduce PARSE (Provenance-Aware Retrieval Sanitization), a domain-aware, fact-preserving sanitization pipeline that classifies each sentence by injection likelihood, extracts structured facts before rewriting, and verifies fact preservation via a consistency-checking loop. A directiveness gate routes 59% of real enterprise documents to a lightweight path, concentrating computational cost on high-risk documents. PARSE achieves 15.6% attack success rate -- a 39% reduction versus the 25.4% baseline -- at 86.9% utility, the largest reduction of any condition evaluated (h=-0.245), nominally significant (p=0.014) but not surviving correction for multiple comparisons. Practitioners should evaluate defenses on domain-matched real documents, not synthetic proxies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables. Camera-ready version. Accepted to the 1st Workshop on Grounding Language Models: Learning Faithfully and Efficiently (GroundLM 2026) at EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:33Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Zeyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EComAgentBench: Benchmarking Shopping Agents on Long-Horizon Tasks with Distributed Hidden Intent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:10:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:40:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T08:51:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:40:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T04:27:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As LLM-based shopping agents enter production, existing benchmarks fail to capture how a shopper's requirements arrive: stated implicitly in the query, recorded in a profile, or revealed only when the right question is asked. Benchmarks that expose full intent upfront and grade only the final choice can neither pose this long-horizon challenge nor explain which requirement an agent missed. To address this gap, we introduce EComAgentBench, a benchmark of 662 tasks grounded in real Amazon products and reviews. Each task scatters these requirements across a visible query, a tool-gated profile, and scripted clarification; an agent must uncover hidden intent, verify candidates against attributes and review evidence, and commit to a single product within 100 tool calls. Moreover, typed, source-tagged rubrics grade every task, attributing each failure to a requirement and its source. Construction is automated yet reliable, with every answer fixed in code before any text is generated and every sample validated. Our evaluation of seven models reveals that even the strongest attains only 57.1% overall accuracy, and rubric satisfaction degrades from visible to hidden sources. Overall, we believe EComAgentBench will serve as a reproducible foundation for moving shopping agents from single-query search toward dependable assistance over long horizons."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures; accepted to the EMNLP 2026 Industry Track (camera-ready version)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:40Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:41:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hsuan","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Chia-Hsuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jui-Ming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jui-Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Refine Hidden States for Reliable LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:03:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T22:30:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:09:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T20:49:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:12:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models show strong reasoning ability, but their internal reasoning process can remain unstable in complex multi-step settings, where early hidden-state errors may propagate to incorrect predictions. We propose ReLAR, a reinforcement-guided latent refinement framework that iteratively updates hidden representations before decoding. ReLAR maintains a compact latent reasoning state and uses learned depth and action controllers to adaptively determine both the number and direction of refinement steps. The controllers are trained with a policy gradient objective based on step-wise likelihood improvement, enabling efficient input-dependent reasoning without explicit chain-of-thought generation. Experiments on medical, mathematical, multi-hop reasoning, and open-ended generation benchmarks show that ReLAR improves accuracy, generation quality, and reasoning stability with substantially lower inference overhead than explicit reasoning baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code is available at tongyu0924/Learning-to-Refine-Hidden-States"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T03:08:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhjot","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Prabhjot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhushan","familyName":"Pawar","name":"Pawar, Bhushan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhu","familyName":"Reddiboina","name":"Reddiboina, Madhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajvee","familyName":"Sheth","name":"Sheth, Rajvee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Not Truly Multilingual: Script Consistency as a Missing Dimension in VLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T04:29:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:25:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T08:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:32:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-27T05:19:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-30T13:34:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:07:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current multilingual evaluations for Vision-Language Models (VLMs) assume a one-to-one mapping between language and orthography, overlooking billions of users of multi-script languages. We introduce PuMVR (Punjabi Multimodal Visual Reasoning), a benchmark of 1,000 strictly parallel image-text instances across Punjabi's three active scripts: Gurmukhi, Shahmukhi, and Roman. Evaluating 10 state-of-the-art VLMs, we expose a substantial and systematic Script Gap. Models frequently solve visual tasks in one script while failing identical tasks in another, with accuracy deltas reaching 16%. Crucially, visual input boosts absolute performance uniformly yet does not close the orthographic gap. Furthermore, cross-script in-context transfer is highly brittle, exposing script-locked knowledge representation. Supported by McNemar tests across all script pairs, our findings demonstrate that current \"multilingual\" VLMs are not truly multi-script. We propose the Script Consistency Rate (SCR), which falls as low as 24.8% on our benchmark, as a mandatory metric for script-agnostic evaluation to ensure equitable AI access. Data and code are available at: https://github.com/prabhjotschugh/Not-Truly-Multilingual-PuMVR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:15:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranavi","familyName":"Kotta","name":"Kotta, Pranavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linlin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Linlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David A.","familyName":"Clausi","name":"Clausi, David A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Securing Multi-Agent GIS Systems: Risk Evaluation and Prompt Hardening Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-13T03:15:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T07:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:31:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lgrs.2026.3726972","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic systems are increasingly integrated with geographic information systems (GIS), where multi-agent coordination enables complex conversational and spatial analysis but introduces security risks. This work presents a security-oriented framework for risk identification, evaluation, and mitigation in a multi-agent GIS system while maintaining adaptability to broader agentic architectures. We test the agentic system of a commercial geospatial partner while developing a modular state-machine-based orchestration framework that abstracts agent behavior into reusable components. We evaluate robustness using a red-teaming framework with an adaptive attacker LLM and a deterministic judge that produces binary outcomes with supporting rationales across multi-turn attacks. We further improve resilience with a prompt optimization framework that treats prompts as structured signatures and injects adversarial demonstrations, enabling systematic security improvements without degrading task performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Kyle Gao and Pranavi Kotta contributed equally to this work. Accepted for publication in IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:16Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:25:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ander","familyName":"Alvarez","name":"Alvarez, Ander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhiya","familyName":"Rajan","name":"Rajan, Santhiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Genuardi","name":"Genuardi, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Wirjadi","name":"Wirjadi, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Mugel","name":"Mugel, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Román","familyName":"Orús","name":"Orús, Román","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProvenanceGuard: Source-Aware Factuality Verification for MCP-Based LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T10:47:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:51:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-27T16:15:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-28T01:05:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-using LLM agents increasingly use the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to answer from heterogeneous evidence sources, including search, APIs, databases, clinical records, and formulary tools. Standard factuality metrics usually test whether an answer is supported by pooled evidence, missing a provenance-sensitive failure mode: a claim may be supported somewhere while being attributed to the wrong source. We call this cross-source conflation.\n We introduce ProvenanceGuard, a source-aware verifier for MCP-grounded answers. It consumes captured MCP traces with stable tool IDs, source IDs, and raw outputs; decomposes answers into atomic claims; routes claims to source-specific evidence; checks support with NLI and a token-alignment proxy; compares stated attribution with the routed source; and returns per-claim verdicts plus an answer-level allow/block decision. Blocked answers can be repaired with retrieval-augmented answer revision and re-verified.\n We evaluate on 281 medical-domain MCP-agent traces. A 266-trace adjudicated subset yields 2,325 LLM-assisted claim labels split by trace; 361 held-out labels are human-verified. On the 40-trace held-out split, ProvenanceGuard achieves block F1 0.802 and source accuracy 0.858 over 260 source-eligible claims, outperforming source-blind baselines that do not emit claim-to-source IDs. On a harder multi-source benchmark it reaches block F1 0.846, while source-plus-relation accuracy drops to 0.229, showing that exact source ownership remains difficult with semantically close sources. Repair-and-reverify resolves all blocked answers in the full trace set, often via conservative fallback. In 50 controlled clinical conflation probes, ProvenanceGuard detects all injected attribution swaps with no retained wrong attribution. These results show that source attribution is an independent axis for factuality verification in MCP-based agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:45:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Phan","name":"Phan, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaochu","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Qiaochu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Pathological VLMs with Cross-scale Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:49:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:20:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T02:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:19:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T15:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-25T01:02:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-27T16:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:46:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pathological images are inherently multi-scale, requiring pathologists to integrate evidence from global tissue architecture at low magnification to cellular morphology at higher magnification for accurate diagnosis. While existing pathological datasets for vision-language models (VLMs) include various scales, they often lack explicit cross-scale reasoning objectives. This limitation prevents VLMs from capturing essential cross-scale representations and learning evidence-based reasoning. To bridge this gap, we introduce the first cross-scale training and evaluation paradigm that formulates pathology interpretation as multi-magnification reasoning. However, creating such a task reveals a critical challenge: multi-image visual question answering (VQA) is prone to text-only shortcuts, which allow models to guess answers using magnification-dependent artifacts rather than visual evidence. To address this, we propose a leakage-aware curation pipeline that combines adversarial text-only screening with constraint-guided question design. Using this pipeline, we construct Scale-VQA, a high-quality benchmark with 4,685 multiple-choice questions grounded in 2,537 pathology images across multiple magnification levels. Finally, we present ScaleReasoner-R1, a model trained via reinforcement learning to optimize performance on cross-scale VQA tasks. ScaleReasoner-R1 achieves state-of-the-art performance on our cross-scale reasoning benchmark and generalizes to SOTA performance on established single-scale benchmarks. Findings suggest that even the limited cross-scale supervision can significantly improve pathological understanding. Code is available at https://github.com/iMVR-PL/ScaleReasoner-R1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MICCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T04:01:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17981","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17981","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17981","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nongyu","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Nongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiaoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Planning to Hammer: Difficulty-Aware Decomposition for Automating Rocq Proofs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:33:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:58:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:53:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:12:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As AI-generated code proliferates, formal verification, particularly through interactive theorem provers such as Rocq and Isabelle, becomes increasingly important for ensuring software correctness. However, producing machine-checked proofs in such provers remains a bottleneck. Existing solutions bring complementary strengths to proof automation: large language models (LLMs) can propose high-level proof strategies but lack local rigor, while automated tactics such as CoqHammer can reliably discharge many local goals but lack long-range planning capabilities. To combine the best of both worlds, we present Quarry, a planning-based proof synthesis framework that separates proof planning from proof execution. Specifically, Quarry asks an LLM to actively propose multiple proof decompositions with arbitrary sublemmas, type-checks them in Rocq under temporarily admitted sublemmas, and ranks candidates using a proof-state-based difficulty model that estimates hammer solvability. It then recursively proves sublemmas within a bounded budget, effectively turning long proofs into sequences of hammer-solvable obligations. We implement Quarry on top of SerAPI and CoqHammer and evaluate it using multiple frontier LLMs across multiple benchmarks. The experimental results show that planning-based decomposition with solvability-aware ranking substantially improves automation while maintaining predictable cost. Under a uniform 10-minute wall-clock budget, Quarry improves over the strongest baseline by 7% to 13% in success rate across three Rocq benchmarks. These results demonstrate that reliable proof automation can be achieved by coordinating neural planning with symbolic execution rather than replacing either."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 8 figures; submitted to OOPSLA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17981","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:51Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:55:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"McFadyen","name":"McFadyen, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ole","familyName":"Jorgensen","name":"Jorgensen, Ole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Coppock","name":"Coppock, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cozmin","familyName":"Ududec","name":"Ududec, Cozmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Inference Compute Shapes Frontier LLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:40:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T15:12:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T16:03:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-17T00:57:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI evaluations are shifting toward harder tasks that benefit from longer trajectories involving tool use and iterative problem solving. As a result, performance is increasingly sensitive to the amount and allocation of compute available at test time (\"inference compute\"). Yet many evaluations still report performance at a single restrictive budget, meaning that low scores may reflect the evaluation setup rather than the model's underlying capability. To test this, we evaluate up to 12 frontier language models on seven challenging benchmarks spanning software engineering, mathematics, medicine, and cybersecurity. We use a controlled setup combining three simple inference-scaling interventions: larger token budgets, context compaction, and repeated submission attempts, guided either by the model itself or by minimal correctness feedback. We find three main results. First, larger token budgets substantially improve performance on benchmarks across multiple domains, including cybersecurity, FrontierMath, Humanity's Last Exam, and TerminalBench. Second, fixed-budget evaluations can increasingly understate frontier capability as models advance. Newer models reach higher performance at large budgets, where they unlock harder tasks and solve them more reliably. Third, benchmarks differ in which inference-scaling methods help most: repeated submission broadly improves performance, but the value of larger token budgets, external feedback, and parallel attempts varies by benchmark. Overall, our results show that benchmark scores are protocol-dependent. We therefore argue that evaluations should report capability as a function of inference-time compute, specify protocol choices explicitly, and compare model generations over a large shared compute range at matched budgets, especially in safety- or policy-relevant settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-17T02:38:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jasmine","familyName":"Brazilek","name":"Brazilek, Jasmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Christoph","name":"Christoph, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maheep","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Maheep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Tullio","name":"Tullio, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carol","familyName":"Kline","name":"Kline, Carol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miles","familyName":"Tidmarsh","name":"Tidmarsh, Miles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arturs","familyName":"Kanepajs","name":"Kanepajs, Arturs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your AI Travel Agent Would Book You a Bullfight: An Agentic Benchmark for Implicit Animal Welfare in Frontier AI Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T06:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:31:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T17:01:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-06T20:56:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:09:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Previous research has evaluated animal welfare using question-and-answer benchmarks. This study investigates whether these evaluations also hold in agentic settings. The agents may showcase different behaviors compared to stand-alone large language models, as demonstrated in prior studies. This work introduces \\textit{TAC (Travel Agent Compassion)}: the first agentic benchmark for assessing animal exploitation. TAC evaluates AI agentic behavior in travel booking scenarios across six animal categories, using thirteen hand-authored scenarios that vary by price, rating, and position, expanded via four augmentation variants into $52$ prompts and run for three epochs, giving $156$ scored observations per model. Nine frontier models across five model families were evaluated.. The results indicate that models tend to prefer harmful scenarios, performing below the random chance rate of $65\\%$ for selecting a neutral booking option, with Claude $4.8$ achieving the highest performance at $64.7\\%$. To address this issue, the persona of an ethical-brand identity was infused into the system prompt, resulting in welfare rates increasing from $32$ to $80$ percentage points, with a mean of $53$ across all nine models. No evidence of evaluation awareness affecting the results was found, based on an Inspect Scout audit of $3,120$ transcripts. These findings are directly relevant to the EU General-Purpose AI Code of Practice, which identifies non-human welfare as a systemic risk. TAC provides a practical method for measuring this risk."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-08T02:14:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18089","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18089","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18089","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjing","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin Q.","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Martin Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuekai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Yurochkin","name":"Yurochkin, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor W.","familyName":"Killian","name":"Killian, Taylor W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruslan","familyName":"Salakhutdinov","name":"Salakhutdinov, Ruslan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric P.","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Eric P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Reasoning Traces to Reusable Modules: Understanding Compositional Generalization in Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:55:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-05T17:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:44:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training pipelines that combine supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with reinforcement learning (RL) have emerged as the key recipe for transforming large language models (LLMs) into robust reasoners. We argue that this combined success is driven by compositional generalization, which we formalize through a hierarchical latent selection model. In this framework, reasoning traces are generated by a cascade of discrete latent selection variables corresponding to reusable atomic modules, including both skills (local operations) and routing mechanisms (how intermediate information is selected, reused, and composed). Within this model, we theoretically show that SFT and RL play asymmetric, complementary roles: SFT supplies the raw module materials in compositional traces, and RL decomposes those traces to identify the latent atomic modules and enable compositional generalization. We design controlled experiments to validate this theory. Our results demonstrate that RL can extract atomic modules from compound traces supplied by SFT and recombine them to solve new configurations. Moreover, we find that training on compound traces yields stronger generalization than training on isolated atomic modules. Finally, we investigate the relationship between SFT and RL data and identify an effective protocol in which SFT ensures coverage of all atomic modules through compositional traces, while RL focuses on novel compositions outside the SFT support to drive exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18089","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:12Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T05:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17128","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17128","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17128","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zhihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbing","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Hongbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke Ztz","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Luke Ztz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songping","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Songping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shift-Left High-Level Synthesis Verification via Knowledge-Augmented LLM Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T17:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T04:32:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:25:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:17:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-Level Synthesis (HLS) relies on transforming original C specifications into synthesizable HLS-oriented C (HLS-C) implementations. Functional consistency verification between original C specifications and HLS-C implementations is a critical yet labor-intensive task in HLS design flows. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently shown promise in automated testbench generation, their stochastic nature often leads to insufficient coverage, inconsistent verification environments, and unreliable equivalence checking results. To address these limitations, we propose a knowledge-augmented, agent-driven shift-left verification framework for automated functional consistency checking between original C and HLS-C implementations before synthesis. The framework introduces a Dual-Tier Consistency Checking mechanism that jointly enforces static structural alignment and dynamic behavioral equivalence between paired testbenches, while integrating symbolic execution and coverage-driven refinement to improve verification completeness. Furthermore, we construct a heterogeneous HLS Verification Knowledge Graph to provide topology-aware reasoning priors for testbench generation, and design an autonomous verification agent to orchestrate iterative refinement and failure diagnosis across heterogeneous toolchains. Experimental results on 107 HLS benchmark pairs demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves 0.9826 average coverage and 0.9533 dynamic consistency, outperforming representative AST-based, retrieval-augmented, and iterative agent-based baselines. https://github.com/cz-5f/HLS-LeVeri.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17128","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:51:03Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:51:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:23:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longlong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Longlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zedi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaopeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chunming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniPlan: An Adaptive Framework for Timely and Near-Optimal Network Planning Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:06:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:29:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Network planning optimization is a fundamental problem across diverse domains, including transportation systems, communication networks, and power grids. It requires simultaneous optimization of multiple competing objectives under complex constraints. Existing network planning optimization frameworks rely on mixed integer programming (MIP) solvers, heuristics, and deep reinforcement learning (DRL) models to compute planning decisions. However, they lack effective adaptability to diverse and dynamic user intents, thus leading to the trade-off between execution time and optimality. In this paper, we propose OmniPlan, an adaptive framework that achieves both timeliness and near-optimality in network planning optimization. To achieve the adaptability lacking in existing solutions, OmniPlan employs a large language model (LLM)-based interpreter to convert heterogeneous natural-language intents into a unified and quantifiable user-preference vector. Then it employs a mixture-of-experts architecture that integrates MIP solvers, heuristics, and DRL models as specialized experts, where OmniPlan adapts to diverse intents by dynamically selecting timely and near-optimal experts. Finally, it incorporates a DRL-based expert configuration module that fine-tunes optimization objective weights to align planning decisions with user-specific preferences. We evaluate OmniPlan with a representative real-world workload, i.e., distributed machine learning (ML), where we leverage OmniPlan to offload a wide spectrum of ML inference tasks, e.g., decision trees, SVM, naive Bayes, XGBoost, and random forests, onto a network of hardware devices. Our experiments on a real-world testbed indicate that OmniPlan achieves near-optimal and low-execution-time offloading for real-world ML inference tasks, reducing latency by up to 97.8\\% and network device resource consumption by up to 11.5\\%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM KDD 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17639","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17639","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17639","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basura","familyName":"Fernando","name":"Fernando, Basura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ERQA-Plus: A Diagnostic Benchmark for Reasoning in Embodied AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:56:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:37:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:18:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generalist embodied agents require more than object recognition: they must reason about spatial relations, actions, procedures, human intentions, environmental constraints, and commonsense consequences from situated visual observations. Yet existing visual and embodied question answering benchmarks often provide limited control over the reasoning dependencies being tested, making it difficult to distinguish grounded embodied reasoning from shortcut-driven visual or linguistic pattern matching. We present ERQA-Plus, a diagnostic benchmark for reasoning in embodied AI. ERQA-Plus contains 1,766 question-answer instances grounded in 711 robot-centric images and organized according to a structured taxonomy spanning perceptual, action-centric, social-interaction, navigation-environmental, and contextual commonsense reasoning. The dataset is constructed using a multi-stage generation and validation pipeline that combines taxonomy-guided question generation, automatic quality judging, iterative revision, and human assessment to improve visual grounding, answer validity, and reasoning quality. We benchmark representative general-purpose vision-language models and embodied models, including LLaVA-NeXT-8B, Prismatic-7B, MiniCPM-V-4.5-8B, Qwen3-VL, RoboRefer-8B, and RoboBrain2.5-8B. Although the strongest model, Qwen3-VL-32B, achieves 83.4% overall accuracy and 61.4 SBERT score, category-level results reveal persistent weaknesses in spatial reasoning, procedural reasoning, event prediction, and intention inference. ERQA-Plus therefore provides a fine-grained evaluation framework for measuring not only whether embodied agents answer correctly, but also which forms of embodied reasoning they can and cannot perform reliably. The dataset is available https://huggingface.co/datasets/huggingdas/erqa-plus and the project page at https://github.com/LUNAProject22/erqa-plus."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17639","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lubin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sixue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sixue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiale","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Jiale","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MapSatisfyBench: Benchmarking Satisfaction-Aware Map Agents through Behavior-Grounded Implicit Decision Factors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:14:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T07:21:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model agents are increasingly integrated into map services. Since map services are embedded in everyday-life scenarios rather than professional task settings, users often express their needs informally, resulting in underspecified queries with many unspoken needs, namely, implicit decision factors that are critical for user satisfaction. Although clarification is an effective way to mitigate this issue, it increases user burden in daily interaction, and a capable agent should first proactively recover such factors from available information sources. However, evaluating this ability is challenging. The first challenge is to determine which implicit decision factors are suitable for evaluation. A factor is evaluable only if it affects user acceptance and can be recovered from information available to the agent before it responds. Second, user satisfaction cannot be reliably represented by a single reference answer, requiring a benchmark that converts satisfaction-relevant factors into objective and quantifiable evaluation targets. To address these challenges, we propose a restore-identify-filter framework that reconstructs complete user needs from behavior-chain evidence, identifies implicit decision factors, and retains only those supported by pre-query evidence. Building on this methodology, we construct MapSatisfyBench from large-scale, real-world anonymized user data and annotate ground truth from five dimensions and enables full-chain evaluation of satisfaction-aware map agents. Experiments show that current agents generally perform well on explicit task completion, but remain limited in satisfying implicit decision factors and proactively acquiring the evidence needed for satisfaction-aware decisions. These findings establish MapSatisfyBench as a benchmark for shifting map-agent evaluation from task completion toward satisfaction-aware spatial decision making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:27:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanjie","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Nanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kaiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvolveNav: Proactive Preflection and Self-Evolving Memory for Zero-Shot Object Goal Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:56:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-Shot Object-Goal Navigation (ZS-OGN) requires embodied agents to explore and locate target objects without any prior training. To this end, recent methods leverage foundation models. But they typically rely on static priors and lack adaptation, which leads to repeated errors and costly trial and error. In this paper, we propose a self-evolving ZS-OGN framework that enables continuous test-time improvement. Specifically, we build an agentic rule memory by extracting actionable knowledge from past trajectories. Then, we propose a retrieval strategy based on upper confidence bound, selecting effective rules by balancing semantic relevance and historical success. In addition, we introduce a memory-guided preflection module that forecasts potential outcomes before action, reducing inefficient exploration. Extensive experiments show that our method outperforms existing zero-shot baselines, achieving a 10.1\\% improvement in success rate with fewer unnecessary steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:15:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18203","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18203","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18203","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zechen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zechen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Palangi","name":"Palangi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Graef","name":"Graef, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. Ali","familyName":"Heydari","name":"Heydari, A. Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon A.","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Simon A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ray","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ray","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeinab","familyName":"Esmaeilpour","name":"Esmaeilpour, Zeinab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Schenck","name":"Schenck, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chloe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chloe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yamin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Menglian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Menglian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"McDuff","name":"McDuff, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lindsey","familyName":"Sunden","name":"Sunden, Lindsey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Malhotra","name":"Malhotra, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shwetak","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Shwetak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed A.","familyName":"Metwally","name":"Metwally, Ahmed A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RubricsTree: Scalable and Evolving Open-Ended Evaluation of Personal Health Agents across Health Memory and Medical Skills"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:34:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:09:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The LLM-empowered personal health agents with user health (sensor) metrics have offered a promising pathway to alleviate global disparities in healthcare access. However, large-scale clinical deployment remains constrained by an open-ended evaluation bottleneck: physician annotation is reliable but costly and unscalable, while LLM-as-a-judge evaluators are scalable but subjective, inconsistent, and sometimes clinically misaligned. We introduce RubricsTree, a scalable evaluation framework with an expert-aligned hierarchical taxonomy of over 100 atomic, clinically-verifiable Boolean rubrics, evolving from the insights of 4,000 real user queries through an iterative human-in-the-loop curation protocol with an expertise panel led by an experienced physician. A context-aware adaptive router activates only the relevant auto-weighted rubric subset per query, providing the throughput needed for scalable evaluation with expert-aligned quality. Through a systematic meta-evaluation, we show that RubricsTree (i) substantially exceeds a strong large-scale evaluation baseline in expert alignment on challenging open-ended queries; (ii) reliably penalizes contextually degraded responses; and (iii) when used as structured instructions, text feedback, or training rewards for performance optimization, yields up to ~66% relative gains on HealthBench for Gemini, GPT, and Qwen model families. RubricsTree thus provides a scalable, auditable, and evolving evaluation infrastructure required for the continuous optimization of product-level personal healthcare AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18203","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simeng","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Simeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yebo","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Aishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing Is Not Screening: Multimodal Hidden Instruction Attacks on Agent Skill Scanners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:29:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agent skills are emerging as an important attack surface in LLM-based systems. Through an empirical study of existing skill scanners, we find that current defenses primarily rely on textual descriptions, manifests, and source code as the main signals for security analysis, which can leave visually conveyed malicious intent insufficiently examined. This creates a practical blind spot: harmful operational instructions hidden in images may bypass scanning while still being recoverable by multimodal agents during deployment. To systematically investigate this threat, we propose SkillCamo, a document-mediated multimodal instruction attack that conceals malicious instructions within images bundled with a skill while rewriting the surrounding documentation to naturally reference those images as part of the normal workflow. Thus, the attack does not rely on the image alone, but on the joint interpretation of textual guidance and visual payload at execution time. To defend against such attacks, we further propose ExecScan, an execution-grounded multimodal scanning module that performs intent extraction, behavior reconstruction, abuse assessment, and deliberative execution simulation over skill artifacts. ExecScan jointly analyzes documentation, code, referenced resources, and visual content to recover hidden instructions, reconstruct executable behavior chains, and identify downstream risks such as exfiltration, destruction, persistence, deception, and privilege escalation. Extensive experiments show that image-hidden malicious instructions challenge existing skill scanners, while ExecScan can improve the skill scanning performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:37Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abir Ashab","familyName":"Niloy","name":"Niloy, Abir Ashab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Ryan","name":"Ryan, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imamul Hossain","familyName":"Rafi","name":"Rafi, Imamul Hossain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md","familyName":"Erfan","name":"Erfan, Md","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rayhanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Rayhanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Source Cybersecurity Logs: An ATT\u0026amp;CK-Labeled Dataset and SLM Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T17:21:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-stage cyberattacks span system, network, and browser logs. Detecting them requires correlating events across all three sources. Machine learning methods can learn these cross-source patterns, but they need labeled multi-source data. Existing public datasets fall short. Network-only datasets such as CICIDS and UNSW-NB15 miss host and browser activity. Host-focused datasets such as LMDG and CICAPT-IIoT lack browser telemetry. ATLAS includes all three sources but labels events only as malicious or benign, without MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge (ATT\u0026amp;CK) technique granularity. No public dataset combines all three sources with per-entry ATT\u0026amp;CK technique labels. We close the gap by building a multi-source log dataset of 870 sessions (70 attack, 800 benign) and approximately 2.3 million events. We captured system, network, and browser activity simultaneously on Windows endpoints. We labeled malicious events with ATT\u0026amp;CK technique IDs, covering 12 tactics and 53 techniques. We generated all attack data using real tools, including Remote Access Trojan (RAT), Command and Control (C2) tunnels, and cloud exfiltration. To demonstrate learnability, we fine-tuned three Small Language Models (SLMs) (Qwen2.5-1.5B, Llama-3.2-3B, Phi-4-Mini) using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). We compared each against its base variant across ten metrics on two tasks: chunk classification and ATT\u0026amp;CK technique identification. Fine-tuning improved every model on every metric. Chunk classification accuracy rose from approximately 8% in the base variants to between 90% and 97% after fine-tuning. Technique identification remained challenging, with the best exact-match accuracy at 42%, although high partial-match scores show the models captured most of the underlying reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:14:26Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:14:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:14:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michèle","familyName":"Finck","name":"Finck, Michèle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Measurement Gap in the Automation of EU Law: Benchmarking Doctrinal Legal Reasoning under the EU AI Act"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models now produce legal text of at least median quality, yet no existing benchmark can evaluate whether they perform doctrinal legal reasoning, which forms the interpretive core of legal work, rather than the ancillary, paralegal tasks that most current legal-AI evaluations measure. This measurement gap is not only methodological but legal: the EU AI Act makes \"appropriate accuracy\" a binding requirement for high-risk AI used in the judicial domain, yet that requirement cannot acquire operational content without the very doctrinal-reasoning benchmark the field lacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bianca","familyName":"Emmerich","name":"Emmerich, Bianca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Yvonne","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu Yvonne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitris","familyName":"Spathis","name":"Spathis, Dimitris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"McDuff","name":"McDuff, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cecilia","familyName":"Mascolo","name":"Mascolo, Cecilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WEQA: Wearable hEalth Question Answering with Query-Adaptive Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:45:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:07:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models are remarkably capable at medical question answering, in some cases surpassing the accuracy of general physicians. However, answering questions about wearable health data remains challenging and understudied, as these ubiquitous sensors produce continuous, high-dimensional, and longitudinal data, which is non-trivial to align with text-centric distributions in LLM pretraining. The diversity of sensor modalities and user intents cannot be effectively handled by a fixed reasoning workflow or a single pretrained foundation model. To address these challenges, we propose WEQA, a query-adaptive agent framework that unifies LLM reasoning with specialized wearable analytical and modeling tools. An LLM controller is employed to synthesize execution plans and dynamically route each query to the appropriate combination of sensor analysis and pretrained models, and perform grounded response auditing with external knowledge. We also curate a benchmark spanning four open wearable datasets comprising analytic and predictive tasks in three different health domains. Experiments show that our framework is 24% more accurate than LLM and agentic baselines, and a blinded study with 12 medical experts and 8 users shows substantial gains in usefulness and clinical soundness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:13:27Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:13:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:13:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18103","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18103","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18103","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah J.","familyName":"Kim-Baumann","name":"Kim-Baumann, Noah J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torsten","familyName":"Hiltmann","name":"Hiltmann, Torsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HistoRAG: Embedding Historical Methodology in Retrieval-Augmented Generation Through Critical Technical Practice"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.7; J.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T16:03:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:04:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the prevailing architecture for grounding language model outputs in external evidence, yet its dominant evaluation paradigms and default configurations remain oriented toward factual question-answering. For interpretive disciplines such as historical studies, RAG embeds assumptions that conflict with scholarly practice. We introduce HistoRAG, a framework that translates historiographical principles into concrete architectural interventions. Separated retrieval and generation decouples source discovery from interpretation, temporal windowing enforces balanced source representation across the research period as a methodological requirement of historical inquiry, and LLM-as-judge evaluation makes relevance judgments transparent and contestable. We evaluate these interventions using SPIEGELragged, applied to 102,189 articles from Der Spiegel (1950-1979). Each intervention addresses a measurable deficiency in standard RAG: era-specific vocabulary retrieves zero chunks from the 1950s when using 1970s terminology, evidence of the temporal skew that motivates windowing; vector similarity and LLM-assessed relevance correlate only weakly (Spearman rho = 0.275), motivating post-retrieval evaluation; and keyword-based and semantic retrieval surface largely disjoint source pools, motivating an architecture in which both operate as complementary retrieval layers under a shared LLM evaluation filter. We also introduce the concept of Zwischentexte (intermediate texts that function as interpretive proposals rather than findings) as a framework for responsible integration of LLM-generated text into scholarly practice. The architecture offers a model for how domain-specific epistemological commitments can be translated into RAG design decisions, and may transfer to other interpretive disciplines working with large corpora."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 6 figures. Companion preprint to a Journal of Digital History notebook article (under review)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18103","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:12:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18075","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18075","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18075","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Antong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chunping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Framework for Context-Aware and Relation-Aware Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792720","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a paradigm for enhancing large language models (LLMs) with external knowledge, yet existing graph-based methods face a fundamental limitation: entity-centric and chunk-centric approaches operate on representations anchored to original text without true knowledge fusion. While entity-centric methods connect logically related content and chunk-centric methods preserve context, both retrieve information separately through similarity search, missing emergent understanding from their synthesis. In this paper, we propose HyGRAG, a hierarchical graph RAG framework that transcends source documents by addressing three core challenges: constructing summaries that genuinely integrate contextual and relational information, leveraging these synthesized representations to access emergent knowledge during retrieval, and efficiently updating hierarchical structures for dynamic corpora. Specifically, we design hierarchical index structures over hybrid graphs with both chunk and entity nodes, then iteratively cluster them and generate LLM-based summaries. Then, we design context and relation-aware retrieval that searches across all abstraction levels while expanding through community membership. Moreover, we enable dynamic knowledge update through attachment-based algorithms with only local re-summarization. Experimental results show that HyGRAG improves the average accuracy of multi-hop reasoning tasks by 9.7%, while maintaining reasonable efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at The ACM Web Conference 2026 (WWW '26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18075","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.18051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.18051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueping","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xueping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Skill Routing for LLM Agents: Decompose, Retrieve, and Compose"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T15:27:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T01:02:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents increasingly rely on external skills -- reusable tool specifications -- but real-world tasks often require composing multiple skills, not just selecting one. We formalize this as the Compositional Skill Routing problem: given a complex user query and a large skill library, decompose the query into atomic sub-tasks, retrieve the appropriate skill for each sub-task, and compose an executable plan. We present SkillWeaver, a decompose-retrieve-compose framework combining an LLM task decomposer, a bi-encoder skill retriever with FAISS indexing, and a dependency-aware DAG planner. To support evaluation, we introduce CompSkillBench, a benchmark of 300 compositional queries over 2,209 real MCP server skills spanning 24 functional categories, sourced from the public MCP ecosystem. Our experiments reveal that task decomposition quality is the primary bottleneck: standard LLM decomposition reaches only 34.2% category recall at the step level. To address this, we propose Iterative Skill-Aware Decomposition (SAD), a retrieval-augmented feedback loop that iteratively aligns decomposition with available skills. SAD improves decomposition accuracy from 51.0% to 67.7% (+32.7%, Wilcoxon p \u0026lt; 10^-6) in a single iteration; DA-conditioned analysis confirms that correct granularity is the prerequisite for effective retrieval (CatR@1 rises from 34% to 41% when DA=1). SkillWeaver reduces context window consumption by over 99%, and transfer experiments confirm generalization (+35.6% relative DA gain even when target categories are absent from the retrieval pool)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:11:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:11:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:11:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17958","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17958","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17958","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Visual Cues: CoT-Enhanced Reasoning for Semi-supervised Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T14:10:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semi-supervised medical image segmentation has emerged as a dominant research problem in medical image analysis, mitigating annotation scarcity by leveraging consistency regularization on unlabeled data. However, existing approaches operate predominantly via visual pattern matching, relying heavily on pixel-level similarities. This visual-centric dependency often falters in clinical scenarios characterized by the visual-semantic mismatch, where visually similar lesions warrant distinct diagnostic conclusions, thus failing to capture the underlying diagnostic logic used by experts. To address this, we move beyond visual cues and propose CERS (CoT-Enhanced Reasoning Segmentation), a framework that integrates Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to distinguish pathologically distinct cases. Specifically, we construct a knowledge pool enriched with linguistic reasoning descriptions generated by large language models (LLMs). A semantic-aware reference selection strategy is introduced to identify historical evidence, filtering candidates first by morphology, and then refining them via CoT consistency to eliminate hard negatives. Furthermore, a multi-scale coordinate attention module (MCAM) is designed to effectively fuse this reasoning-derived context into the decoding process. Extensive experiments demonstrate the superiority of CERS against state-of-the-art approaches, particularly in resolving boundary ambiguities and semantic inconsistencies. The code is available at https://github.com/cymasuna/CERS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MICCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17958","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:09:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:09:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PreAct: Computer-Using Agents that Get Faster on Repeated Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:40:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-using agents drive real software through the screen -- clicking and typing -- but they solve every task from scratch: asked to repeat a task, an agent re-reads the screen, re-reasons every tap, and pays the full cost again. We present PreAct, which lets such an agent get faster on tasks it has done before. The first time it succeeds, PreAct compiles the run into a small state-machine program-states that check the screen, transitions that act-and on later runs replays it directly instead of invoking the agent 8.5-13x faster, with no per-step language-model calls. Replay is not blind: at each step PreAct checks that the screen matches what the program expects before acting, and hands control back to the agent the moment something is off.\n PreAct applies the same discipline when deciding what to keep: a freshly compiled program enters the store only if, re-run from a clean state, an independent evaluator confirms it solved the task-catching programs that replay to their last step yet leave the task undone. Across a mobile, a desktop, and a web benchmark, this store-time check separates repeated runs that improve from ones that degrade as faulty programs accumulate, worth 1.75-2.6 tasks per benchmark, the same direction on all three; a fallback that explores afresh when no program fits brings PreAct level with a strong record-and-replay baseline. We also report what did not matter: prompt wording, runtime guardrails, and whether a language model or a plain embedding retriever selects which program to reuse."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:43Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aueaphum","familyName":"Aueawatthanaphisut","name":"Aueawatthanaphisut, Aueaphum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Badri Raj","familyName":"Lamichhane","name":"Lamichhane, Badri Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trustworthy Self-Composable Big-Data-as-a-Service: An LLM-Orchestrated Multi-Agent Framework for Automated Data Engineering, AutoML, MLOps Deployment, and Drift-Aware Lifecycle Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:34:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:54:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Big-Data-as-a-Service (BDaaS) platforms require re liable automation across data ingestion, cleaning, feature engi neering, model development, deployment, and post-deployment monitoring. However, existing LLM-based data science agents and AutoML systems mainly focus on isolated workflow stages, leaving limited support for lifecycle-level orchestration, artifact governance, human oversight, and drift-aware adaptation. This paper proposes a trustworthy self-composable BDaaS frame work based on LLM-orchestrated multi-agent collaboration. The proposed architecture decomposes the BDaaS lifecycle into specialized agents for data ingestion, data cleaning, feature engineering, AutoML training, model evaluation, MLOps de ployment, monitoring, and drift detection. A central LLM or chestration layer coordinates agent execution, validates interme diate outputs, manages workflow context, and enables dynamic workflow composition. The framework also incorporates shared artifact governance, reproducibility support, human-in-the-loop checkpoints, and drift-aware feedback loops. A prototype-based evaluation is conducted using controlled tabular benchmark datasets with missing values, categorical variables, outliers, class imbalance, and simulated covariate drift. Compared with manual ML, AutoML-only, and single-agent LLM baselines, the pro posed multi-agent BDaaS pipeline achieves competitive predictive performance while improving lifecycle-level reliability, including workflow completion, artifact traceability, deployment readiness, reproducibility, and drift recovery. The results suggest that LLM-orchestrated multi-agent systems can extend conventional AutoML toward trustworthy, adaptive, and production-oriented BDaaS lifecycle automation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17905","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17905","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17905","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peixian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Peixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaorui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyan","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Xueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChLogic: Evaluating Robustness of Logical Reasoning in Chinese Expressions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:28:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:53:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models perform increasingly well on standardized logical reasoning benchmarks, but whether this ability remains robust beyond English is unclear. We introduce ChLogic, an English--Chinese aligned benchmark that tests whether models preserve logical reasoning performance when the same latent logical structure is expressed in English and diverse Chinese surface realizations. Built from formal logical templates, the benchmark contains three data sets: (i) the General aligned set, derived from 60 General Propositions across nine template families; (ii) the Difficult aligned set, derived from 40 Difficult Problems; and (iii) the Chinese-only set, covering 15 language-specific phenomenon types. Each aligned item pairs one English reference expression with five Chinese realizations. Experiments on Qwen3, Ministral, and GLM models reveal a persistent English--Chinese performance gap. Back-translation from standard Chinese into English often improves performance on the General aligned set, but produces mixed effects on the Difficult aligned set, where Qwen3-32B and GLM-5.1 perform worse after translation. These results indicate that Chinese surface realization, translation artifacts, and model-specific behavior jointly affect multilingual logical reasoning. Overall, ChLogic provides a useful stress test for the robustness of multilingual reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17905","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:08:11Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:08:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:08:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17890","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17890","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17890","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shicheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shasha","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Shasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenghao","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Zenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huawei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Huawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Rollout Editing for Reducing Overthinking in RL-Trained Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T13:10:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:52:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-form chain-of-thought reasoning can improve LLM performance on complex tasks, but models often continue generating unnecessary reasoning after a correct answer has emerged. We refer to this behavior as overthinking. We study this phenomenon from the perspective of GRPO-style reinforcement learning (RL) post-training, framing it as a training-time credit-assignment problem rather than merely a decoding-time stopping problem. In rollouts sampled at the onset of GRPO training, we observe that successful trajectories can exhibit a slightly higher degree of overthinking than unsuccessful trajectories for the same prompts. This early imbalance provides a starting point for an undesirable feedback loop: because GRPO assigns sequence-level credit, it cannot distinguish the solution-reaching prefix from the unnecessary continuation that lengthens a successful trajectory. Both receive positive update signal, allowing the initial imbalance to grow into more severe overthinking during training. To address this issue, we introduce Dynamic Rollout Editing (DRE), a training-time intervention for successful trajectories that continue thinking after answer emergence. DRE preserves the accepted verified prefix, edits the remaining thinking, and prefers the edited trajectory within the same RL group, weakening the preference signal for unnecessary thinking without penalizing the reasoning needed to reach the answer. Experiments across diverse tasks show the effectiveness of DRE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 10 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17890","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:07:52Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:07:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:07:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rean Clive","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Rean Clive","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Fehring","name":"Fehring, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theresa","familyName":"Eimer","name":"Eimer, Theresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marius","familyName":"Lindauer","name":"Lindauer, Marius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Feurer","name":"Feurer, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Environment-Grounded Automated Prompt Optimization for LLM Game Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T12:06:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:49:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents in interactive environments are highly sensitive to their prompts, yet prompt engineering remains a manual, task-specific process. We introduce an automated prompt optimization framework for LLM agents that decomposes the observation-to-action pipeline into a goal-conditioned descriptor agent and an action selection agent, and iteratively refines each module's prompt through an LLM-driven evolutionary loop guided by environment returns. We propose a behavior analyzer to attribute episode outcomes to specific prompt components, and a mutator to propose targeted revisions to the prompt, before validating them through environment rollouts. We evaluate on all five BabyAI tasks in the BALROG benchmark, comparing our pipeline against BALROG's RobustCoTAgent under both plain and guided prompt initializations. Optimization improves performance consistently across tasks and conditions, without requiring updates to the model weights. On PutNext, a multi-step coordination task where the RobustCoTAgent achieves 0% success, our framework reaches up to 72.5% success rate using the same underlying LLM with optimized prompts. These results suggest that a multi-agent framework, combined with automatic prompt optimization, enhances LLMs without the need for fine-tuning or extensive human supervision."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:06:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kairui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kairui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengsheng","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Shengsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Changsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiveStarPro: Proactive Streaming Video Understanding with Hierarchical Memory for Long-Horizon Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:18:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:47:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the remarkable progress of Video Large Language Models (Video-LLMs), current online architectures still struggle to simultaneously process continuous video streams, decide autonomously when to respond, and preserve long-horizon contextual memory. These obstacles undermine real-time responsiveness and cause severe forgetting throughout prolonged interactions. In this work, we introduce LiveStarPro, a live streaming assistant that is designed for proactive video understanding over long-horizon streams. The design of LiveStarPro rests on three complementary components. The first component is Streaming Verification Decoding (SVeD), an inference framework that identifies the appropriate response timing through single-pass perplexity verification, thereby eliminating the dependency on explicit silence tokens. The second component is Streaming Causal Attention Masks (SCAM), a training strategy that enforces incremental video-language alignment over variable-length streams. The third component is Tree-Structured Hierarchical Memory (TSHM), a recursive memory architecture that organizes evicted historical information into event chains and consequently enables efficient retrieval from effectively unbounded video streams. To facilitate a comprehensive evaluation under realistic online conditions, we further present OmniStarPro, a large-scale benchmark that spans 15 diverse real-world scenarios and that extends to hour-scale streams for the assessment of long-term recall. Extensive experiments demonstrate that LiveStarPro consistently surpasses existing methods, attaining a 28.9% improvement in semantic correctness and an 18.2% reduction in timing error, while its streaming key-value cache further yields a 1.58x inference speedup over the same model without caching. The model and the code are publicly available at https://github.com/sotayang/LiveStarPro."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17789","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17789","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17789","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofie","familyName":"Kamber","name":"Kamber, Sofie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Diebold","name":"Diebold, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Riachi","name":"Riachi, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stella","familyName":"Brogna","name":"Brogna, Stella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Gloster","name":"Gloster, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Wampfler","name":"Wampfler, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mind Companion: An Embodied Conversational Agent for Process-Based Psychotherapy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T11:05:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:46:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ichi69079.2026.00123","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Access to evidence-based psychotherapy remains limited worldwide, with long waitlists even in high-income regions. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer potential for scalable mental health support when designed with clinical oversight and safety mechanisms. We present Mind Companion, an LLM-based embodied conversational agent integrating multi-layered psychological analysis with process-based therapy principles. The system performs real-time analysis of client statements across fact extraction, psychological flexibility process detection, emotion recognition, and safety monitoring. Analysis results are stored for supervising clinicians to inform therapeutic planning. Response generation incorporates retrieval-augmented generation from evidence-based therapeutic literature and context-aware prompting. Responses are delivered through an embodied avatar with synchronized speech synthesis and animation. We evaluated three LLM configurations (GPT-4.1-mini, GPT-5.2, Claude Sonnet 4.5) against therapist responses from real therapy sessions using automated LLM-judge assessment and expert evaluation with 11 professional psychotherapists. GPT-5.2 achieved higher ratings than human therapist responses across understanding, interpersonal effectiveness, collaboration, and therapeutic alignment in both evaluations, demonstrating the feasibility of LLM-based conversational agents as tools to complement clinical care."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17789","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-17T02:05:41Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:05:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:05:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faqiang","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Faqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cijun","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Cijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Qibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shattering the Autoregressive Curse: Dynamic Epistemic Entropy Orchestrated Erasable Reinforcement Learning for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:55:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:43:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although reinforcement learning (RL) has expanded the cognitive boundaries of large language models (LLMs), it often remains vulnerable to the autoregressive curse in long-horizon logical reasoning: small epistemic perturbations introduced early in generation can propagate irreversibly along the Markov decision process flow, triggering cascading failures that drive the reasoning trajectory toward collapse. To overcome this autoregressive cascade, in which a single early mistake can compromise all subsequent reasoning steps, we propose dynamic epistemic entropy orchestrated erasable reinforcement learning ($\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$). $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ eliminates reliance on external signals by grounding the model's endogenous local autoregressive cross-entropy as an intrinsic coordinate of epistemic uncertainty. By introducing segment-level adaptive dynamic thresholds and advantage allocation, $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ enables the model to precisely excise localized logical defects while reusing historical key-value (KV) cache streams, thereby endowing the reasoning process with a self-healing capability. We train $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ on the DeepMath-103k dataset. Experimental results show that $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ reshapes the exploration efficiency of long-sequence reasoning and improves sample efficiency while maintaining linear memory overhead. On mathematical reasoning benchmarks such as AIME, $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ achieves substantial performance gains, with the 4B and 8B parameter models surpassing previous state-of-the-art (SOTA) results by 5.349\\% and 6.514\\%, respectively. These findings suggest that $\\text{E}^3\\text{RL}$ shatters the autoregressive curse in long-sequence reasoning and establishes a theoretical and systems-level foundation for the next generation of self-healing artificial general intelligence (AGI)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Takacs","name":"Takacs, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Bracher","name":"Bracher, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svitlana","familyName":"Vakulenko","name":"Vakulenko, Svitlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding and Debugging Failures in N-Gram-Based Generative Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T09:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:42:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Retrieval (GR) is an emerging Information Retrieval (IR) paradigm that is motivated by increasingly capable language models. In GR, a model directly generates identifiers for relevant documents. While these systems offer unique advantages, they also introduce distinct failure mechanisms. We explore these failure modes in three contributions: (1) We present a taxonomy of GR failure modes based on GR literature. (2) We empirically investigate failure in a subset of GR: ngram-based methods, more specifically, SEAL and MINDER. Our analysis reveals common issues, such as ambiguous docids, low identifier diversity, and the disproportionate impact of specific identifiers. (3) We introduce a new web-based tool that helps the IR community analyze generated ngrams and their respective contribution to the final ranking, providing an intuitive interface to identify where such GR methods go wrong."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:04:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinhong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yinhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Trainee to Trainer: LLM-Designed Training Environment for RL with Multi-Agent Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:48:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning pipelines for Large Language Model (LLM) training often rely on manually redesigned environments between stages, requiring practitioners to heuristically infer which configuration will best improve the current policy. To automate this process, we propose the LLM-as-Environment-Engineer framework in which the current policy model analyzes failure trajectories together with contextual information and proposes modifications to the next-stage training environment configuration. We also introduce MAPF-FrozenLake, a controllable testbed whose generator exposes multi-dimensional environment configurations, making it suitable for studying and benchmarking environment redesign. On this testbed, we condition the environment engineer on structured summaries of policy behavior, failure cases, and environment statistics, from which it produces the configuration for the next training stage. With Qwen3-4B as the backbone, our framework achieves the strongest aggregate performance on our benchmarks, outperforming larger proprietary LLMs (e.g., GPT, Gemini) and fixed-environment training baselines. We further analyze which forms of context are most effective, finding that successful environment updates rely on failure evidence and preserve configurations that already work. Interestingly, the current RL checkpoint serves as a better environment engineer than the original base model, suggesting that policy learning improves the model's ability to diagnose its remaining weaknesses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:19Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Songze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanzi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EnvRL: Learn from Environment Dynamics in Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:48:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:39:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for training Large Language Models (LLMs) as agents. However, conventional RL methods for long-horizon agentic tasks often struggle with sparse outcome rewards. Intuitively, this overlooks the rich environment dynamics information contained in rollout interaction trajectories. We argue that the interaction experience inherently serves as an implicit supervision signal, reveals the underlying transition mechanisms of the environment, and enables the agent to construct a more accurate internal model of the environment.. Therefore, in this work, we investigate how to leverage this additional signal to improve policy learning. Specifically, we propose EnvRL, a framework that incorporates environment dynamics learning into agentic RL via two auxiliary objectives: state prediction and inverse dynamics. By jointly optimizing with the primary RL objective, we encourage the agent to internalize environment dynamics from its own interaction experience. Extensive experiments on two long-horizon agentic benchmarks demonstrate that EnvRL achieves significant improvements on success-rates over RL-only baselines, e.g., when trained with GRPO, lifting Qwen-2.5-1.5B-Instruct from 72.8% to 77.4% on ALFWorld, and from 56.8% to 67.0% on WebShop."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Komamizu","name":"Komamizu, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ichiro","familyName":"Ide","name":"Ide, Ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeSRPA: Decoupled Speech Role-Playing Agent via Inference-Time Intervention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T08:30:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized text-based role-playing, creating immersive Speech Role-Playing Agents (SRPAs) requires a seamless bridge between cognitive reasoning and paralinguistic nuances. Current SRPAs primarily rely on end-to-end (E2E) fine-tuning. However, this paradigm suffers from poor generalization to unseen characters due to its reliance on role-specific data, while imposing a \"modality alignment tax\" that degrades intrinsic LLM reasoning capabilities.\n We propose DeSRPA, an agentic framework for character role play via inference-time intervention on frozen backbones. DeSRPA employs a dual-level control vector mechanism, Internal Cognitive Steering and External Expressive Rendering, to synchronize \"mind\" and \"voice\". Experiments on SpeechRole and OmniCharacter benchmarks demonstrate that DeSRPA significantly outperforms E2E baselines in personality and emotional consistency. It achieves high speech naturalness, narrowing the gap with proprietary models like GPT-4o Audio, while remaining a scalable and training-free paradigm."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:03:00Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:03:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:03:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengkun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Perturbation for Reliable LLM Evaluation over Comparison Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:44:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating large language models (LLMs) is important for understanding their capabilities, comparing competing systems, and supporting the deployment of reliable models in practice. For open-ended tasks, pairwise evaluation has become a popular paradigm, in which two responses to the same prompt are compared and the resulting judgments are aggregated into an overall ranking. A central challenge of this paradigm is intransitivity: the induced comparison outcomes may fail to support any coherent global ranking. For example, one may observe cyclic preferences such as $A \\succ B \\succ C \\succ A$, or inconsistencies involving ties such as $A \\equiv B\\equiv C\\neq A$. Such contradictions make the resulting leaderboard unstable and challenging to interpret. In this paper, we propose a prompt perturbation framework for improving the consistency of pairwise LLM evaluation. Our approach generates perturbed variants of each prompt, uses the resulting comparison graphs to identify and filter out structurally inconsistent comparison patterns, and then applies standard ranking methods to the filtered comparisons. A key feature of the proposed framework is that graph-level structural consistency is incorporated explicitly into the evaluation pipeline before ranking aggregation. This provides a simple and principled way to reduce cyclic inconsistencies and improve the reliability of LLM rankings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sottovia","name":"Sottovia, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Torcinovich","name":"Torcinovich, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oswald","familyName":"Lanz","name":"Lanz, Oswald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Divide, Deliberate, Decide: A Multi-Agent Framework for Fine-Grained Egocentric Action Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:31:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:35:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-grained action recognition in egocentric video is challenging for Vision-Language Models (VLMs): actions often differ only in small visual cues, and a single model tends to be biased toward a subset of these cues. We propose Divide, Deliberate, Decide, a fully-local, zero-shot multi-agent framework in which (i) a VLM orchestrator chunks the video and proposes a top-k candidate label list per segment, (ii) an ensemble of heterogeneous VLM specialists, drawn from different open model families, engages in a structured deliberation that includes a peer-consultation round of questions, and (iii) agent rankings are aggregated with a Borda count and the orchestrator re-ranks its own prediction in light of the specialists' evidence. The entire pipeline runs locally with no fine-tuning. Experiments show that our method positively improves zero-shot action recognition performance over the baseline, highlighting the influence of a heterogeneous deliberation step, showing that the gain stems from decorrelated model priors rather than from additional compute."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:02:05Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:02:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:02:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zesheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zesheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshuo","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Tianshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Benchmark Illusion: Pruned LLMs Can Pass Multiple Choice but Fail to Answer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T07:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:34:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compressing large language models reduces memory use and inference cost, but it can also create failures that standard benchmarks miss. A pruned model may still perform well on multiple-choice evaluations, yet fail to answer the same question in open generation. We ask what pruning changes: does it erase the correct answer, or does it make the answer harder to produce as the top output?\n We study this question with multilingual question answering, tracking the same questions before and after pruning. We find a benchmark illusion. Under high-sparsity pruning, especially Wanda, models often fail in greedy open generation while still selecting the correct answer under multiple-choice scoring. In these recognition-only errors, the answer is usually not gone, but demoted: it often reappears with beam search, sampling, or one in-context example. Overall, multiple-choice benchmarks can overstate the usability of compressed LLMs, creating an evaluation blind spot. Compressed models should be tested on what they can produce, not only on what they can recognize."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17591","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17591","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17591","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guanghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Peiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Closing the Feedback Loop: From Experience Extraction to Insight Governance in Verbal Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:55:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training-free verbal reinforcement learning enables LLM agents to learn from world feedback -- objective signals such as dynamic task outcomes, market returns, or demand forecasts -- by extracting verbal rules from experience and injecting them as context, updating the agent's behavior without parameter changes. However, in non-stationary environments these agents face a retention-forgetting dilemma: retaining stale insights causes negative transfer, while discarding them causes catastrophic forgetting when conditions recur. We identify four requirements for navigating this dilemma -- outcome-driven evaluation, persistent structured evidence, non-monotonic knowledge lifecycle, and compositional governance -- and show that existing methods invest heavily in experience extraction while underinvesting in insight governance. We propose a three-layer architecture -- rules, evidence, and skills -- connected by a feedback-driven curation loop that closes the governance gap. Rules capture distilled experience from world outcomes; evidence logs track each rule's reliability across episodes; skills govern which rules to apply, how to resolve conflicts, and when to abstain. On financial forecasting as a case study, where world feedback is naturally abundant, noisy, and non-stationary, we show that the same accumulated experience either degrades performance below the zero-shot baseline or dramatically improves accuracy and risk-adjusted returns, depending on whether the curation loop is present."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the ICML 2026 RLxF: Reinforcement Learning from World Feedback Workshop, RLxF@ICML 2026, Seoul, South Korea"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17591","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:01:19Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:01:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepInsight: A Unified Evaluation Infrastructure Across the Physical AI Stack"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:22:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating a Physical AI stack spans operators that differ by more than three orders of magnitude -- from a single foundation-model decoding step to thousands of physics ticks of whole-body control -- varying orthogonally in modality, reward semantics, and resource profile. No existing framework spans this range, so the stack is evaluated today by stitching together separate harnesses that share neither runtime nor scoring, preserving each segment's local validity but losing the shared identity needed to diagnose cross-layer regressions. We present DeepInsight, an evaluation infrastructure that serves this full spectrum on a single runtime. Rather than homogenize the regimes, it preserves their heterogeneity behind three narrow abstractions -- task, resource, and result -- each realized as one invariant shared by every subsystem: one episode driver, one resource-handle protocol implemented by every expensive backend (LLM inference and sandboxed runtimes alike), and one trace identity scheme under which every event is written. Deployed in production across all three layers of an embodied humanoid stack, this single set of invariants onboards new benchmarks largely by configuration. Where mature peer orchestrators exist -- at the foundation-model end -- it reproduces published references and peer-framework readings within their own spread, runs the same suites faster on a single node, and scales near-linearly across nodes. Its distinctive return is diagnostic: because every layer writes into one shared trace, a regression that begins in one layer and surfaces in another stays localizable on that trace -- a cross-layer payoff no federation of per-segment harnesses can reproduce."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duling","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Duling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangzheng","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Bangzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jidong","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Jidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cordon: Semantic Transactions for Tool-Using LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T06:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:32:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tool-using LLM agents are shifting the unit of computation from explicit human-issued commands to model-driven tasks with stateful consequences. Yet today's agent runtimes still expose tools as isolated RPCs. This interface gives runtimes a convenient integration point, but it lacks a task-scoped execution boundary for commit, rollback, recovery, and audit across multi-step agent workflows. We argue that this mismatch calls for a runtime containment boundary rather than another per-call guardrail. This paper introduces Cordon, a transactional runtime system for staging and validating irreversible agent effects before commit. A semantic transaction is a task-level execution boundary that binds tool intents and runtime-tracked result lineage to reversible local state, staged external effects, delegated authority, and audit metadata. Cordon implements this abstraction with a transaction manager that tracks derived result objects, executes reversible mutations in shadow state, stages outward-facing actions in an effect outbox, and records recovery metadata. The runtime then validates the composed execution flow before it commits state or releases external effects. Our evaluation across adversarial and benign workflows shows that Cordon exposes cross-step violations missed by existing defenses. It also reduces irreversible-effect failures while preserving benign task completion with modest approval and latency overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17546","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17546","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17546","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congjie","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Congjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyi","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changshui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Changshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEAGym: An Evaluation Environment for Self-Evolving LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:30:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-evolving LLM-based agents improve mainly by changing their agent harness: the structured execution layer around a base model, including prompts, memory, tools, middleware, runtime state, and the model-tool interaction loop. Existing evaluations often reduce this process to isolated task scores or a single sequential curve, obscuring whether an update produces reusable improvement, overfits recent tasks, increases cost, or harms older behavior. We introduce SEAGym, an evaluation environment for measuring agent harness updates across training, validation, test, replay, and cost records. SEAGym turns Harbor-compatible benchmarks into dynamic self-evolution task sources with train batches, frozen update-validation, held-out ID and OOD transfer views, replay diagnostics, and saved snapshot and metric records. Instantiating SEAGym on Terminal-Bench 2.0 and HLE, we compare ACE, TF-GRPO, and AHE under a shared epoch/batch protocol. The results show that these evaluation views provide complementary signals about the evolution process: frequent updates may fail to improve held-out performance, useful intermediate snapshots may collapse later, and source diversity and model backend can affect harness reliability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17546","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengqian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chengqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhua","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Wenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingcai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Bingcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuqin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiquan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniDrive: An LLM-Choreographed Multi-Agent World Model with Unified Latent Co-Compression for Multi-View Driving Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T05:25:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:29:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative world models for autonomous driving face two unresolved tensions: heterogeneous control injection, where free-form language, HD-maps, trajectories, and camera poses reside in incompatible representational spaces, and post-hoc cross-view fusion, where per-camera latents fail to encode global 3-D geometry. We trace both to a single root cause: the absence of a shared symbolic interlingua aligning language, geometry, and pixels at the latent-token level. We present DRIVE-CHOREO, an LLM-choreographed multi-agent world model that recasts controllable multi-view video generation as latent choreography. Three Qwen2.5-VL agents - a Director parsing user intent into a structured WorldScript, a Cartographer grounding it into spatially-anchored layout tokens, and an Auditor feeding cross-view critiques back as auxiliary supervision - jointly author a single position-aware token sequence. This sequence is co-compressed with the multi-view video via a view-time permutation that enforces inter-camera geometry within the convolutional receptive field of a 3-D VAE. On nuScenes, DRIVE-CHOREO sets new state-of-the-art multi-view consistency and BEV mAP (21.6) with competitive FVD (45.7); a detector trained purely on our synthetic data gains +2.4 NDS on the real validation split, validating downstream utility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T02:00:02Z","registered":"2026-06-17T02:00:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T02:00:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kellen","familyName":"Gillespie","name":"Gillespie, Kellen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robyn","familyName":"Perry","name":"Perry, Robyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Enterprise Agent Routing: Degradation, Diagnosis, and Recovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T04:55:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Production LLM assistants route user requests to growing libraries of specialized tools, but how does routing accuracy degrade as the catalog scales? We study single-step routing on a 110-agent, 584-tool catalog from a deployed enterprise productivity assistant, evaluating three frontier models from 10 to 110 agents. Routing F1 on under-specified requests drops 16--23 percentage points across models. An oracle analysis decomposes the degradation into a \\emph{retrieval} gap (the model cannot surface the right tool) and a \\emph{confusion} gap (even with perfect retrieval, the oracle ceiling drops 10pp). Embedding-based shortlisting recovers +10--11pp F1 at full scale across all three models and two providers. A production annotation study (1,435 human-labeled utterances, three annotators) confirms the recovery on real traffic at +10--17pp despite 10--15pp lower absolute performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages (6 main + 4 appendix), 4 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:59:39Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:59:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:59:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Huizi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenkong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Guangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxian","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jingxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiying","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent CY","familyName":"So","name":"So, Kent CY","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan YP","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Bryan YP","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun Kwok","familyName":"Wing","name":"Wing, Yun Kwok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yanqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhou","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIPatient Arena: EHR-grounded evaluation of large language models in end-to-end clinical consultation workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:35:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly considered for use in clinical consultation tasks, yet most medical evaluations remain static, single-turn, or narrowly outcome-based, limiting their ability to reflect the sequential, uncertain, and interactive nature of real-world care. Here, we propose AIPatient Arena, an EHRs-grounded evaluation framework for assessing the clinical utility of LLMs across eight dimensions of clinical competence. The framework integrates EHR data into patient-specific knowledge graphs, enabling multi-turn physician-patient interactions. We applied AIPatient Arena on a primary cohort of 437 patients and two out-of-distribution validation cohorts of 119 and 67 patients. We observe that LLMs performed well in medical interview questioning skills (QS; mean scores, 4.43-4.99/5), ethical and professional conduct (ET; 4.38-4.93/5), and clarity and transparency of clinical explanations (EX; 3.80-4.72/5). Performance was moderate in information integration (II; 3.19-4.21/5) and medication safety and justification (MS; 3.13-3.78/5), but persistent weaknesses were observed in handling of ambiguous patient responses (HR; 2.57-3.32/5), information coverage (IC; 2.08-3.02/5), and diagnostic accuracy and reasoning (Dx; 2.63-3.55/5). Process-based evaluation revealed recurrent interaction failures, including repetitive questioning, omission of past medical history, and inadequate handling of uncertainty. Richer conversational context improved diagnostic reasoning but yielded limited gains in treatment planning. These findings indicate that final-answer accuracy alone is insufficient for evaluating clinical readiness and highlight the importance of assessing how models gather, interpret, and communicate information throughout a consultation. AIPatient Arena provides an EHR-grounded framework for workflow-oriented pre-deployment evaluation of medical LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"49 pages, 12 figues, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqing","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xueqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfei","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Lingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuohan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhuohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can LLMs Be CEOs? Benchmarking Strategic Resource Reallocation with Multi-Role Agent Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:20:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating the decision-making capabilities of large language models (LLMs) is a growing research priority, yet existing benchmarks focus on isolated cognitive tasks such as reasoning, knowledge retrieval, and economic rationality in stylized settings. These evaluations overlook the defining challenge of real executive decision-making: integrating conflicting recommendations from specialized stakeholders under information asymmetry, organizational constraints, and temporal dependencies. We introduce \\textsc{CEO-Bench}, a multi-agent benchmark that evaluates LLMs on CEO-level strategic resource reallocation -- the process of redirecting capital across business units in a multi-round, constraint-rich organizational environment. In \\textsc{CEO-Bench}, LLM agents receive conflicting advice from four role-conditioned C-suite advisors (CFO, CTO, COO, CMO), each with private signals and distinct priorities, and must synthesize these into a concrete allocation plan evaluated along four dimensions: role integration, conditional boldness, history-sensitive judgment, and plan validity. Experiments across five frontier models on 13 scenarios reveal that all models achieve high structural validity but diverge sharply on strategic calibration -- the hardest capability layer. We identify systematic failure modes including single-advisor capture, conservative default under ambiguity, and historical amnesia, and uncover a structural integration-boldness tradeoff: models that engage more deeply with conflicting perspectives tend to produce less decisive action. These findings delineate the current capability boundary of LLMs as organizational decision-makers and inform the design of future AI-assisted executive systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiya","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ICBCBench: An Industry Consortium Benchmark for Financial Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:19:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:24:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Deep Research Agents in knowledge-intensive domains such as finance, establishing reliable and domain-aligned evaluation standards remains a critical challenge. Existing benchmarks focus on either closed-ended question answering or open-ended report evaluation, failing to jointly capture retrieval-reasoning accuracy and end-to-end research quality required in real-world workflows. We introduce ICBCBench, a consortium-driven benchmark for financial deep research, developed in collaboration with domain experts from a broad range of financial institutions and academia, involving over 50 experts across more than 40 organizations. It adopts a dual-track paradigm integrating objective tasks with verifiable answers and subjective long-form report evaluation, enabling complementary assessment of retrieval-reasoning accuracy and end-to-end report quality in terms of expert alignment, citation consistency, and source quality. Experiments on state-of-the-art DRAs and large language models reveal substantial gaps in complex reasoning, factual grounding, and report quality, highlighting the challenges of achieving industry-level performance. Our dataset and evaluation framework are available at https://github.com/DeepFin-Intelligence/ICBCBench."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 14 figures. Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:21Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zehang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamin","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MODE-RAG: Manifold Outlier Diagnosis and Energy-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T03:06:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (M-RAG) enhances Large Vision-Language Models, it remains highly susceptible to cross-modal hallucinations, causal fabrications, and sycophancy. Furthermore, existing mitigation pipelines often face an intervention paradox: static rules tend to unnecessarily disrupt accurate generations, whereas leaving the multi-modal reasoning completely unguided allows existing mismatches to cascade into severe logical fabrications. To quantify and mitigate these hallucinations, we propose a Multi-Agent system, MODE-RAG, driven by Variational Free Energy (VFE) and internal attention states to dynamically gate interventions. High-risk queries are routed to five stage-specific agents, integrating Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for rigorous causal derivation and logit perturbations to penalize sycophancy. Dedicated Correction and Overseer agents ensure formatting stability and perform post-hoc factual verification. To objectively evaluate our approach, we introduce ModeVent, a challenging subset derived from the MultiVent dataset. Extensive experiments indicate that our system effectively reduces hallucination rates and logical fabrication, significantly improving the robustness of M-RAG systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be presented at ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:58:09Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:58:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quoc-Duy","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Quoc-Duy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh-Tuan","familyName":"Vo","name":"Vo, Anh-Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung-Nghia","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Trung-Nghia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Silhouette-Guided Animal Art"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T02:24:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative AI has advanced the ability to render photorealistic or artistic images, yet it remains limited in a key aspect of human creativity: interpreting ambiguous shapes. This phenomenon, rooted in pareidolia, allows humans to perceive meaningful forms in random patterns such as clouds, stones, or leaves. To computationally replicate this imaginative process, we introduce Visual Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Visual-RAG), a framework that generates animal art directly from natural silhouettes. Our method retrieves structurally similar animal shapes from a curated corpus of 28,586 high-quality silhouettes and uses them as reference exemplars to guide diffusion-based generation with ControlNet and IP-Adapter. Ablation studies confirm that shape Context with RANSAC provides the most accurate alignment, while removing shape standardization reduces the inlier ratio to just 13.4\\%, underscoring the importance of structural fidelity in Visual-RAG. A user study with 12 participants evaluated the outputs in terms of aesthetics, silhouette fidelity, and overall impression. Results reveal that while Visual-RAG provides plausible interpretations, challenges remain in achieving high perceptual impact. This work lays the foundation for computational pareidolia, showing how machines can contribute to the early stages of imaginative discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SOICT 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:57:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:57:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:57:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17391","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17391","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17391","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Logan","familyName":"Mann","name":"Mann, Logan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Abdur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Saifullah","name":"Saifullah, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taaha","familyName":"Kazi","name":"Kazi, Taaha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasu","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Vasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NarrativeWorldBench: A Frontier-Saturated Benchmark and a Latent World Model for Long-Horizon Co-Creative Audio Drama"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-16T01:04:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:18:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-form serialized audio drama, with arcs that run for 200 to 800 episodes, is a major creative medium and a setting where frontier large language models (LLMs) fail. We benchmark 21 models, spanning classical, fine-tuned, open-frontier, closed-frontier, and reasoning tiers, on a uniform set of structural narrative metrics. All closed-frontier systems saturate at a plot-beat F1 in the band [0.78, 0.81] and collapse by about -0.20 F1 at horizon h=200. We introduce NarrativeWorldBench, an open benchmark of nine narrative-structure metrics evaluated across horizons h in {10, 20, 50, 100, 200}, with cross-lingual evaluation across four Indic languages (Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Marathi). We introduce N-VSSM, a Narrative Variational State-Space Model that maintains a structured 256-dimensional latent world state over more than 200 episodes via a Mamba-2 backbone with an event-conditioned posterior and an 8B decoder. N-VSSM holds plot-beat F1 \u0026gt;= 0.84 across all horizons at 4x lower compute than the closed-frontier band. A learned Cultural Transfer Function lifts cross-language fidelity by +0.20 to +0.23 Likert points. In a within-subjects writer study (n = 12 professional authors, 240 trials), N-VSSM is preferred over Claude Opus 4.5 on long-arc consistency 71% of the time and rated +1.3 Likert points higher on controllability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages. Accepted to the ICML 2026 Workshops on High-dimensional Learning Dynamics (HiLD) and Culture x AI"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17391","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:54Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17368","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17368","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17368","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Deen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taotao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taotao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed General-Purpose Agent Networks: Architecture, Key Mechanisms, and Prototypes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T23:57:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:16:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have accelerated the transition from passive conversational assistants to autonomous agents that can understand goals, plan actions, invoke tools, and execute multi-step tasks. Yet the capability of a single agent remains constrained by its local data, tool permissions, runtime environment, and governance boundary. This paper studies distributed general-purpose agent networks: open peer-to-peer networks in which heterogeneous agents deployed on personal devices, edge nodes, or autonomous computing environments can discover one another, establish trust, negotiate cooperation rules, and execute open-ended tasks. We argue that such networks cannot be obtained by simply combining existing peer-to-peer overlays with conventional multi-agent systems. Unlike traditional P2P networks, agent networks must propagate semantic declarations about intentions, capabilities, states, and cooperation constraints. We therefore propose a layered architecture centered on a protocol adaptation layer that connects upper-level task semantics with lower-level network operations. Based on this architecture, the paper identifies three core mechanism problems: semantic announcement propagation for collaborator discovery, verifiable identity and multi-topic reputation for cooperation governance, and semantic-gradient mechanism design for open task execution. For each problem, we present a technical route, including bodyless gossip with sequential logs, BAID-based identity binding with MG-EigenTrust reputation, and a Stackelberg-style mechanism-generation loop driven by semantic attribution feedback. We further report prototype overhead results for BAID-style tiered verification and mechanism-level simulations of MG-EigenTrust under cross-topic disguise-collusion attacks. The resulting framework provides a system-level foundation for open, trustworthy, and scalable agent collaboration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17368","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:23Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Schmalfuss","name":"Schmalfuss, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Despoina","familyName":"Paschalidou","name":"Paschalidou, Despoina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanja","familyName":"Fidler","name":"Fidler, Sanja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kashyap","familyName":"Chitta","name":"Chitta, Kashyap","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose M.","familyName":"Alvarez","name":"Alvarez, Jose M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DriveJudge: Rethinking Autonomous Driving Evaluation with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T23:39:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:15:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving has shifted towards end-to-end policy learning, where reliable, interpretable policy evaluation is a fundamental challenge as driving quality is highly context-dependent. Commonly used rule-based driving metrics like EPDMS are interpretable but lack context-awareness, while recent VLMbased evaluations are context-aware but limited by ambiguous VLM outputs and weak physical grounding. To evaluate driving in a manner that is both interpretable and context-aware, we introduce DriveJudge. DriveJudge is a driving evaluation agent that combines rule-grounded evaluation with Vision-Language Model (VLM) reasoning and selectively invokes physically-grounded deterministic rule functions after interpreting the environmental context. To train and evaluate DriveJudge, we curate a large-scale dataset of 33,577 challenging driving samples with human annotations on whether the driving behavior is reasonable in the given scenario. With this dataset, we address the underexplored problem of driving metric evaluation, and introduce two human-aligned benchmark tasks: Driving Quality Classification and Trajectory Preference Selection. DriveJudge outperforms EPDMS for driving quality classification by 21.23 AUC, and the recent VLM-based DriveCritic for trajectory preference selection by 6.5%, setting a new standard for interpretable and precise driving evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:56:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:56:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:56:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17328","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17328","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17328","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianxuan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Xianxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenglai","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Shenglai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouren","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shouren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MemTrace: Probing What Final Accuracy Misses in Long-Term Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T22:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:13:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM agents increasingly maintain long-term memory of user facts across sessions. Yet such memory is usually evaluated by aggregating accuracy over question rows or episodes. Because this approach scores question rows independently, even when several questions probe the same fact, it cannot show how that fact behaves as conditions change. We introduce MemTrace, a benchmark whose unit of measurement is the knowledge point: a single typed fact about the user, rather than an individual question. MemTrace probes each fact along three controlled dimensions: memory age, defined by how many sessions ago the fact appeared in the history; question type, covering current state, earlier state, and trajectory of change; and evidence condition, covering present, missing, and contradicted-by-false-premise settings. Evaluating 13 memory-system configurations across four paradigms, we find that similar pooled accuracy hides different failures: recovering a fact's current and earlier states does not imply tracking how it changed, and safe abstention does not imply correcting a false premise. The dominant bottleneck is evidence use, not retrieval: when systems fail, the evidence was retrievable 10 times more often than it was missing. These results suggest that improving long-term memory requires better use of reachable evidence, not simply more storage or retrieval."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17328","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:30Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:55:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baishali","familyName":"Chaudhury","name":"Chaudhury, Baishali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengdie Flora","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengdie Flora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunji Hayley","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Hyunji Hayley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungmin","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae Oh","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Jae Oh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Consistency in LLM Logical Reasoning via Structural Uncertainty"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models can arrive at the same answer through reasoning paths that are unstable, contradictory, or difficult to rank consistently -- a failure mode especially prevalent in multi-step deductive reasoning. Existing methods assess reliability primarily through output dispersion -- measuring how much sampled answers differ -- but this discards a complementary signal: whether the model can consistently rank competing reasoning candidates. We propose structural uncertainty, a consistency-aware framework derived from the stability of self-preference-induced rankings over sampled reasoning solutions. Given a query, we generate multiple candidate solutions and ask the model to judge pairwise preferences among its own outputs. We aggregate self-preferences into ranking distributions via Bradley-Terry modeling with PageRank, and decompose the signal into two entropy-based components: across-trial ranking instability and within-trial candidate ambiguity. Across five LLMs and eight benchmarks, structural signals provide information complementary to answer dispersion: on logical and mathematical reasoning tasks, the combination improves identification of unreliable instances, while on factual retrieval the structural signal collapses toward uniformity, diagnosing a regime boundary where reasoning-level consistency evaluation is uninformative. The two components relate differently to accuracy: within-trial ambiguity correlates positively with correctness -- consistent with settings where multiple plausible solution paths remain competitive -- while across-trial instability correlates negatively, signaling unreliable reasoning. Structural uncertainty is best understood not as a universal confidence estimator, but as a regime-sensitive evaluator of logical reasoning consistency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026 Workshop on Logical Reasoning of Large Language Models. Accepted as best paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:55:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqing","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Text-to-Video Retrieval for Operating Room Clips via Action-Driven Digital Twins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T21:06:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-video retrieval in operating rooms (OR) is an enabling technology for OR safety, as it allows stakeholders to retrieve and inspect recordings of specific events. However, because the most safety-critical events may not follow the common structure, to unlock its full potential text-to-video retrieval must be able to handle implicit queries that require reasoning to identify the right video (e.g., the step right before clipping). However, existing methods rely on global embeddings that cannot reason over such queries. We propose OR3, a text-to-video retrieval method that converts clips into action-driven digital twins (ActDTs), grouping concurrent subject-action-object triplets under non-overlapping temporal intervals. Moreover, rather than cross-modal matching through paired encoders, OR3 performs imagination-based retrieval where an LLM generates hypothetical ActDTs from queries. This enables intra-modal matching via a single encoder trained with ActDT-tailored hard negatives. Finally, evidence-grounded refinement revises imagined ActDTs based on discrepancies with top candidates to capture procedure-specific patterns. We construct a benchmark from MM-OR with 276 implicit queries across four reasoning categories over 386 clips from robotic knee procedures. OR3 achieves 57.6 R@1 and 77.3 R@5, outperforming the strongest baseline. These results demonstrate that OR3 enables fine-grained discrimination between visually similar OR video clips through temporal action reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:50Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:54:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqing","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training LLMs with Reinforcement Learning over Digital Twin Representations for Reasoning-Intensive Surgical VideoQA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T20:40:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:10:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Surgical video question answering requires multi-step reasoning across semantic, spatial, and temporal dimensions. Existing methods architecturally compress videos into discrete token representations and couple visual perception with reasoning. This approach fragments continuous spatial-temporal relationships and has been shown to restrict multi-step reasoning capabilities. We introduce a reinforcement learning (RL) framework that trains large language models (LLMs) to decouple perception from reasoning by operating over digital twin representations constructed from surgical foundation models. Additionally, we introduce hierarchical representations across frame, temporal window, and procedure levels with probabilistic uncertainty estimates. Finally, we propose a novel reward that combines format validation with accuracy assessment through clinical plausibility evaluation and uncertainty-aware calibration for training. To demonstrate the capabilities of this approach, we introduce REAL-Colon-Reason, a colonoscopic benchmark with 2000 question-answer pairs across three complexity levels. We achieve state-of-the-art performance on REAL-Colon-Reason and two existing surgical VideoQA benchmarks REAL-Colon-VQA and EndoVis18-VQA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:54:25Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:54:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:54:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maram","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Maram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savitra","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Savitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hariseetharam","familyName":"Gunduboina","name":"Gunduboina, Hariseetharam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daksh","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Daksh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Haris","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Haris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhasis","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Subhasis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biplab","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Biplab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoDisaster: Benchmarking Orchestrated Agents for Operational Disaster Geo-Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:46:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Remote-sensing vision-language models (RS-VLMs) have advanced Earth-observation analysis toward visual interpretation and instruction-following, yet fall short of operational geo-intelligence, which demands tool-grounded spatial reasoning and structured, evidence-backed decisions. We introduce GeoDisaster, an operational geospatial disaster reasoning benchmark with 2,921 verified instances across 43 question types and five task families: deforestation monitoring, multi-hazard analysis, building-damage assessment, flood-safe routing, and Sentinel-1 SAR flood monitoring. Instances integrate heterogeneous EO/GIS evidence-optical and SAR imagery, raster masks, vector geometries, road networks, and exposure layers-spanning hazard detection, damage assessment, exposure estimation, and diagnostic report generation. Ground-truth answers are grounded in executable geospatial workflows and deterministic consistency checks, removing the need for language-model annotation. We further propose an orchestrated multi-agent framework with 18 disaster-oriented tools, where role-specialized agents coordinate through explicit execution contracts, aligned via Role-Contract Expectation Alignment (RCEA): failure-aware supervised fine-tuning combined with contract-grounded reinforcement learning over dense step-level signals. Experiments show that GeoDisaster challenges existing RS-VLMs and agentic systems, while RCEA improves tool use, evidence grounding, state consistency, and decision generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 11 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:42Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17220","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17220","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17220","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxu","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Mingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenpeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wenpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunbo","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yunbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhunchen","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhunchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guotong","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Guotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Rules Learn: A Self-Evolving Agent for Legal Case Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T19:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:06:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Legal case retrieval remains challenging due to the complexity of legal language and the need for precise lexical alignment between queries and relevant cases. Although dense retrieval models have achieved notable progress, empirical studies show that BM25 continues to serve as a strong baseline in this domain. It motivates us to propose a self-evolving framework for rule-driven query rewriting that enhances BM25 without any parameter training. The framework equips an LLM-based agent with an automatic evaluation environment, enabling it to iteratively create rewriting rules, plan validation experiments over rule combinations, and eliminate ineffective rules based on historical feedbacks. We evaluate our method on the Chinese legal case retrieval benchmark LeCaRD-v2. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework outperforms non-evolutionary baselines, including human-designed rules and greedy rule selection, particularly when powered by a highcapacity core LLM. We also conduct detailed analyses to investigate the mechanisms underlying self-evolution. Our findings reveal that LLM's capabilities to leverage previous experimental results and its intrinsic knowledge of rule elimination play critical roles in refining the rule set via self-evolution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17220","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:53:07Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:53:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17203","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17203","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17203","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Essam","name":"Essam, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kareem","familyName":"Wael","name":"Wael, Kareem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azza","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Azza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Haitham","name":"Haitham, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Soliman","name":"Soliman, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samer","familyName":"Saber","name":"Saber, Samer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Habib","name":"Habib, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trust-Aware Multi-Agent Traceability: Confidence-Calibrated Knowledge Graphs for Consistent Software Artifact Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:41:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:05:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent AI systems are increasingly used to automate software engineering tasks including requirements analysis, architecture design, test generation, and traceability linking. When these agents operate as a sequential pipeline over shared software artifacts, errors and low-confidence decisions made by upstream agents propagate to downstream stages, producing orphaned requirements, contradictory links, and compliance gaps that pose significant risks in safety-critical domains. We propose a trust-aware coordination framework where a shared knowledge graph serves as both centralized semantic memory and a coordination surface through which agents assess and build upon each other's contributions using calibrated confidence scores. Our approach introduces a two-stage traceability link prediction pipeline combining embedding-based retrieval with LLM-based multi-criteria analysis, a traceability seeding mechanism that enables comparison between derivation-time and validation-time confidence, and a consistency protocol governing pipeline interactions through confidence threshold gating, confidence divergence detection, and conflict resolution. We evaluate on an automotive software engineering case study measuring link prediction calibration, protocol effectiveness, threshold sensitivity, and the impact of traceability seeding. Ablation studies confirm that confidence calibration is essential for effective pipeline coordination."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17203","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:44Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Svetlana","familyName":"Lukina","name":"Lukina, Svetlana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamad Al","familyName":"Mdfaa","name":"Mdfaa, Mohamad Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gloria","familyName":"Haro","name":"Haro, Gloria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Zagoruyko","name":"Zagoruyko, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gonzalo","familyName":"Ferrer","name":"Ferrer, Gonzalo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VL-MemKnG: Hybrid Memory with a Spatio-Temporal Knowledge Graph for Question Answering over Long Egocentric Navigation Trajectories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Answering navigation-relevant questions over long egocentric videos requires retrieving and organizing evidence distributed across distant temporal moments while maintaining spatial and contextual consistency. Although long-context vision--language models can achieve strong answer quality, they are computationally expensive for long trajectories and inefficient for repeated querying. Recent graph-based approaches such as VL-KnG address this challenge through persistent spatio-temporal knowledge graphs, but graph-centric retrieval alone may underrepresent broader temporal continuity and contextual cues. We present VL-MemKnG, a hybrid memory framework that extends VL-KnG by combining a spatio-temporal knowledge graph with persistent segment-level contextual memory. The knowledge graph captures structured relational information and long-range object associations, while segment-level memory preserves broader temporal context for long-horizon evidence retrieval. A hybrid retrieval-and-reasoning module jointly operates over both memory representations to produce evidence-grounded answers and temporally organized supporting evidence. We also introduce WalkieKnowledgeT+, an extension of WalkieKnowledge for long-horizon navigation-oriented video question answering. The benchmark includes temporally distributed reasoning tasks requiring evidence aggregation across multiple non-cooccurring moments. On WalkieKnowledgeT+, VL-MemKnG improves Top-1 retrieval accuracy from 58% to 67% and Recall@1 from 34.50% to 40.55%, outperforming all compared methods, including Gemini 2.5 Pro and Qwen 3.5+. The gains are particularly pronounced on temporal-global and temporally scattered aggregation questions, demonstrating the benefits of combining structured relational memory with segment-level contextual memory while maintaining efficient query-time inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:15Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajjad","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Sajjad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verified Detection and Prevention of Concurrency Anomalies in Multi-Agent Large Language Model Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.4; F.3.1; D.1.3; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:19:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent LLM systems share state through memory stores, vector indices, and tool registries. We model such sharing as long-running read-generate-write operations under deterministic-generation semantics -- the regime durable-execution engines enforce by deterministic replay -- and formalize four concurrency anomalies in TLA+: stale-generation, phantom-tool, causal-cascade, and tool-effect reordering, structural analogues of classical isolation anomalies, each with a TLC counter-example. The exclusion lattice over these anomalies is trivial; the contribution is the mechanically verified realizability and strict separation of one maximal chain within it, $L_0 \\subsetneq \\cdots \\subsetneq L_4$, to our knowledge the first machine-checked consistency hierarchy for such runtimes. A development of 274 Verus obligations (zero assume, zero admit; trust base: two structural axioms and a mutex correspondence) proves the detectors sound and complete against the specifications and each runtime its avoidance set. Three deployed Rust runtimes realize L0-L1 (pessimistic locking, serializable snapshot isolation, default-SI), each verified against stale-generation and refined to its state machine; L2-L4 are exec-mode-verified with dependency-free prevention twins (A3, A6, A2: 0/1000 versus 1000/1000), and L2 is run live across three model families (A3 prevented in all 120 retracted sessions). We reproduce a silent lost update in ByteDance's deer-flow, formalizing its fix as a verified $L_0 \\to L_1$ refinement, and exhibit tool-effect reordering in LangGraph's ToolNode on unmodified output, removed by an L3 commit-order sequencer. The verified detector, refinements, and realizability artifacts are the contribution; the phenomena and lattice are classical."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 2 figures, 6 tables. Verus/TLA+ verification artifact, reference Rust runtime, and Python harnesses, plus a supplementary appendix (Sections A-F, Tables S1-S6), included as ancillary files"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadsadegh","familyName":"Abolhasani","name":"Abolhasani, Mohammadsadegh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid Reza","familyName":"Firoozfar","name":"Firoozfar, Hamid Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Mousavi","name":"Mousavi, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Jen-Hwa","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Paul Jen-Hwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Parasocial Scripts to Dyadic Persistence in Autonomous AI-Agent Communities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T18:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:03:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While parasocial interactions (PSIs) and parasocial relationships (PSRs) have been studied in conventional media settings, we investigate whether PSI- (colloquial) relational cues also exist in online communities where both sides are autonomous AI agents. We analyze 4,434 posts and 50,338 comments from Moltbook through three theory-based textual indicators: attachment/intimacy language, reciprocity bids, and self-identification to original poster (OP). The combined results across methods based on keyword matching, few-shot large language model (LLM) annotation, and grouped-context LLM annotation reveal that PSI colloquial cues prevail and are strongly associated with OP re-engagement and a reciprocal reply structure. These results are robust across negative controls, nullification, clustered-standard-error re-estimation, and multiple-testing correction. A dyadic persistence test further affirms reciprocity bids aligned with sustained OP-involving mutual recurrence, providing empirical evidence for bridging interaction-level PSI scripts with PSR-consistent repeated dyadic patterns. We interpret the evidence as a behavioral structure in discourse by LLM-enabled agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted for review in ARR for EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:52:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Tripodi","name":"Tripodi, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reed","familyName":"Naidoo","name":"Naidoo, Reed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah F.","familyName":"McGough","name":"McGough, Sarah F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tapabrata","familyName":"Chakraborti","name":"Chakraborti, Tapabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing, Fusion, and Trustworthiness: A Systematic Evaluation of Foundation Model Representations for Multimodal Cancer Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T09:50:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models (FMs) have emerged as powerful representation extractors for medical data, yet their generalizability to datasets under distribution shift remains underexplored. This work systematically evaluates FM-based representations on a suite of computational pathology tasks across two real-world commercial cohorts, IH-BC and IH-NSCLC, drawn from the licensed in-house (IH) oncology dataset. The analysis focuses on two modalities, whole-slide images and transcriptomic profiles, drawn from the IH multimodal data. We first benchmark unimodal probing performance across five FMs on eight downstream classification tasks, and find that image and omics representations carry complementary predictive signals. Then we investigate whether multimodal fusion can yield additional gains over unimodal baselines by comparing three image-omics fusion strategies built on paired representations. The trustworthiness of selected unimodal and multimodal pipelines is further assessed through conformal prediction. Our results show that FM representations achieve competitive performance on out-of-distribution data and that multimodal fusion helps mainly when no single modality dominates the signal. Conformal prediction reveals that in the majority of cases where a point prediction fails, the true diagnosis remains recoverable within the prediction set, reinforcing the value of uncertainty-aware inference for clinical support."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankyul","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Hankyul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewon","familyName":"Noh","name":"Noh, Jaewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sang","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Sang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yongsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel Waikin Loh","familyName":"Matienzo","name":"Matienzo, Gabriel Waikin Loh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young Il","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Young Il","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ee Wei","familyName":"Seah","name":"Seah, Ee Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akriti","familyName":"Vij","name":"Vij, Akriti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evaluation of Data Leakage Risks in Tool-Using LLM Agents in Realistic Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-15T09:16:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents are increasingly being adopted in enterprise and personal settings with access to emails, databases, documents, and other tools where they can read, update, and disseminate sensitive information. Much of prior research on data leakage risks in agents has focused on adversarial data exfiltration through prompt injections and jailbreaks. However, sensitive information may also be exposed during non-adversarial use, creating leakage risks even when users issue benign requests.\n We report a joint evaluation by the Singapore AI Safety Institute and the Korea AI Safety Institute examining agent data leakage in 12 realistic, non-adversarial tasks spanning customer support, DevOps, web automation, and enterprise and personal productivity. The evaluation covers five risk types: lack of data awareness, audience awareness, policy compliance, data minimization, and access-boundary awareness. Both institutes tested a common set of scenarios mirroring real-world deployments using independent testing environments and task-specific LLM-judge rubrics.\n Across the three tested agents, none achieved fully correct and fully safe execution across all scenarios. Successful task completion often coincided with data-handling failures such as accessing unnecessary information or disclosing information to inappropriate recipients, indicating that capability and data-handling safety should be evaluated separately. Qualitative review also revealed claim-action mismatches, simulation-aware behavior, user-simulator role reversal, and interpretation gaps in automated judging. Overall, the results indicate that operational data leakage is a first-order agent-safety concern distinct from adversarial exfiltration and provide a methodology for future evaluations of agent data-handling safety."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:45Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17104","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17104","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17104","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Usami","name":"Usami, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkatram","familyName":"Vishwanath","name":"Vishwanath, Venkatram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. Wes","familyName":"Bethel","name":"Bethel, E. Wes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prefill/Decode-Aware Evaluation of LLM Inference on Emerging AI Accelerators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T12:23:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in latency- and cost-sensitive settings, inference efficiency has become a central systems challenge. While GPUs dominate current deployments, a growing number of AI accelerators claim advantages for LLM inference, yet it remains unclear under which conditions such accelerators outperform GPUs in practice. Recent inference systems decompose execution into Prefill and Decode phases, which exhibit distinct computational characteristics and latency metrics, commonly captured by time to first token (TTFT) and time per output token (TPOT).\n This paper presents a phase-aware evaluation of LLM inference performance across GPUs and emerging AI accelerators using a common model, Llama2-7B. By separately measuring Prefill and Decode performance, we reveal that accelerator advantages differ by phase and metric. Our results show that GPUs consistently excel in the compute-intensive Prefill phase, while GroqRack achieves significantly lower TPOT during Decode (batching not currently supported). However, GPUs regain an advantage in Decode throughput as batch size increases.\n These findings demonstrate that each platform exhibits distinct phase-dependent strengths. We further analyze heterogeneous Prefill/Decode disaggregation across different accelerator platforms, identifying performance gains and the workload and network conditions under which such gains are realized."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures. Accepted to the Workshop on HPC for AI Foundation Models \u0026amp; LLMs for Science (HPAI4S'26), co-located with IEEE IPDPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17104","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17082","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17082","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17082","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hauteng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hauteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xieyuanli","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xieyuanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benwu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkai","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wenkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParkingTransformer: LLM-Enhanced End-to-End Trajectory Planning for Autonomous Parking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:52:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"End-to-end autonomous parking has emerged as a critical task within the realm of autonomous driving. However, existing methods suffer from black-box characteristics, lacking high-level semantic understanding and interpretability, which impedes the realization of seamless long-distance autonomous parking from the road to the target spot. To address these limitations, we propose ParkingTransformer, a novel framework that leverages multi-view perception and the scene understanding capability of Large Language Models (LLMs). By combining trajectory queries with LLMs implicit state features, our method interacts directly with historical information and raw sensor data to output planning trajectories, eliminating the need for dense Bird's-View (BEV) representations. To compensate for the inadequate spatial reasoning ability of LLMs, we introduce 3D positional encoding to explicitly inject spatial geometric awareness. Furthermore, a fixed-window streaming mechanism is designed for historical information processing, significantly improving long-term temporal processing efficiency and inference speed. Additionally, a coarse-to-fine decoding strategy is employed to progressively enhance trajectory precision. Extensive closed-loop experiments are conducted on the CARLA simulator and real-world vehicle platforms. The results demonstrate that our method achieves a driving score of 61.32 in CARLA simulator and an average success rate of 88.70% in real-world experiments, validating the feasibility and effectiveness of the proposed algorithms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17082","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:50:03Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:50:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.17058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.17058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Changqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Changqing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Shouwei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shouwei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Florida State University"],"givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kai","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Argonne National Laboratory"],"givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Sheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Oregon State University"],"givenName":"Wenqian","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Wenqian","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Coding Agents on SZ-Family Lossy Compression Across Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.4; D.1.3; D.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-22T16:53:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:00:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) coding agents are increasingly applied to code translation and optimization, yet their effectiveness in performance-critical high-performance computing (HPC) settings remains poorly characterized. This paper evaluates LLM-based coding workflows on SZ-family error-bounded lossy compression kernels, which combine numerical constraints with memory-intensive and control-flow-heavy implementations. We study two representative CUDA workloads (SZp and SZx) and target two heterogeneous execution platforms: NVIDIA GPUs and Cerebras wafer-scale accelerators. Focusing on single-agent iterative generation, we analyze not only final throughput but also agent runtime behavior, including iteration patterns, sensitivity to prompt specification, and characteristic failure modes. Our results reveal a pronounced cross-architecture divergence. On GPUs, stronger models can achieve substantially higher throughput but exhibit increased sensitivity to prompt precision and optimization guidance, whereas on Cerebras the dominant challenge lies in producing runnable programs under a PE-centric spatial execution model. We further observe that LLM agents are more effective on modular kernels (SZx) than on tightly coupled bit-level pipelines (SZp), where structural dependencies hinder optimization progress. These findings suggest that evaluating LLM coding agents for HPC requires accounting for both performance outcomes and architecture-specific robustness, and that success on thread-based platforms does not directly transfer to spatial accelerators."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures. Accepted to IPDPS 2026 HPAI4S Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.17058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-17T01:49:32Z","registered":"2026-06-17T01:49:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-17T01:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":73,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2026-06-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2026-06-17T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28agent+OR+agents+OR+reasoning+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+planning+OR+reinforcement+OR+reward+OR+evaluation+OR+benchmark%29+AND+descriptions.description%3A%28LLM+OR+%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+foundation%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}